#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:2 scripts/data-management.en.txt:2
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:2 scripts/definitions.en.txt:2
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:2 scripts/organizing.en.txt:2
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:2 scripts/resourcing.en.txt:2
#: scripts/software.en.txt:2 scripts/standards.en.txt:2
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 1\n"
msgstr "// Slide 1\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation is based on “Principles for data quality” by Arthur Chapman\n"
msgstr "本簡報內容以 Arthur Chapman 所撰之《資料品質原則（Principles for data quality）》為基礎\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:6 scripts/data-management.en.txt:6
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:7 scripts/definitions.en.txt:8
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:7 scripts/organizing.en.txt:8
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:7 scripts/resourcing.en.txt:7
#: scripts/software.en.txt:7 scripts/standards.en.txt:7
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:7
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 2\n"
msgstr "// Slide 2\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:8
#, no-wrap
msgid "During this presentation, we will explore the principles of data quality applied to data capture, specifically when capturing data from collection labels, fieldwork notebooks, spreadsheets, etc.\n"
msgstr "在這場簡報中，我們將探討資料品質原則如何應用於資料擷取階段，特別是處理典藏標籤、田野筆記本、試算表等來源的資料時。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:10 scripts/data-management.en.txt:11
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:12 scripts/definitions.en.txt:18
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:33 scripts/organizing.en.txt:16
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:13 scripts/resourcing.en.txt:15
#: scripts/software.en.txt:12 scripts/standards.en.txt:20
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:12
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 3\n"
msgstr "// Slide 3\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data quality is essential at every step of the data mobilization process, especially in the data capture steps.\n"
"Each person involved in the data capture has a share of responsibility regarding the data quality, but most decisions on this topic quality have to be taken at the institutional level.\n"
"The keywords here are: planning and documentation! \n"
"As mentioned in the Foundations documentation presentation, use existing standards and plan your workflows to match your goals; document everything you can, at every step, and share or re-use documents, data, tools and standards as much as possible.\n"
msgstr "資料品質在整個資料動員流程中的每一個階段都相當重要，尤其是在資料擷取步驟中。每一位參與資料擷取的人，對資料品質皆負有一定責任；然而，與資料品質相關的多數關鍵決策，應於機構層級進行規劃與制定。此處的兩個關鍵詞是：規劃與文件化！\n"
"如同在「基礎概念簡報」中所提及，請善用既有標準、規劃資料工作流程以對應你的目標，並在每個階段記錄所有可以記錄的內容，儘可能分享或重複使用既有的文件、資料、工具與標準。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 4 \n"
msgstr "// Slide 4 \n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:23
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is an example of a data quality workflow.\n"
"In this workflow, it begins with the collection of specimens, moves to data capture, then quality control, publishing and finally use.\n"
"Data quality isn’t the sole responsibility of the first person in the process (here, the collector) -- it is shared at every step and every person in the process should have a responsibility for quality.\n"
"A functioning feedback loop needs to be in place in order to check, complete, update or correct data.\n"
"This is where documentation is essential: you need to know who was responsible for each step in the process in order to validate changes that have been made to the data (or need to be made to the data).\n"
msgstr "這是一個資料品質工作流程（data quality workflow）的範例。\n"
"在這個流程中，從標本採集開始，接著進入資料擷取、品質控制、資料發布，最後到資料使用。\n"
"資料品質並不是由流程中最初的參與者（此處為採集者）單方面負責的，每一位參與者每個階段都承擔資料品質的責任，也都應對品質把關負起一定責任。\n"
"為了能檢查、補充、更新或修正資料內容，必須建立一個可運作的回饋機制。\n"
"這也是為什麼文件紀錄非常重要：你需要清楚知道每個流程步驟是誰負責的，才能在需要修改或已修改資料時，確保其變更是合理且可追溯的。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:25 scripts/data-management.en.txt:35
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:27 scripts/definitions.en.txt:30
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:44 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:44
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:29 scripts/software.en.txt:26
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:53 scripts/structures.en.txt:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 5\n"
msgstr "// Slide 5\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this simplified view of the data flow, you can see some of the data quality responsibilities of each group of people involved.\n"
"In this example, the team in charge of the mobilization can be split into the ‘transcribers’ and ‘curator’ roles.\n"
"The transcribers team needs to ensure data is captured and saved as best as possible, while the ‘curator’ role has the ultimate responsibility for ensuring that each team is fulfilling their roles in the process.\n"
msgstr "在這個簡化的資料流程圖中，你可以看到不同角色在資料品質上的責任分工。\n"
"在此範例中，負責資料動員的團隊可分為兩個角色群組：資料謄錄者與策展人。\n"
"謄錄團隊的任務是盡可能準確地擷取與儲存資料，而策展人則需負起最終責任，確保每個團隊在流程中都確實履行其職責。"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:31 scripts/data-management.en.txt:41
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:33 scripts/definitions.en.txt:34
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:68 scripts/organizing.en.txt:58
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:70 scripts/resourcing.en.txt:36
#: scripts/software.en.txt:34 scripts/standards.en.txt:74
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:28
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 6\n"
msgstr "// Slide 6\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "Once the data workflow is in place, the data capture itself can begin.\n"
"In the following slides, we will explore the different types of information that can be captured from specimens or field observations, and see what are the most common mistakes that should be avoided when dealing with each kind of information.\n"
"The main topics will be as follows: taxonomic information, spatial information, collection information, descriptive information.\n"
"Please note that each occurrence (each row in your database or spreadsheet) should have information linked to these 4 main topics in order to be shared and reused accordingly.\n"
msgstr "當資料工作流程建立完成後，資料擷取工作便可正式展開。\n"
"在接下來的簡報中，我們將依序探討可從標本或田野觀察中擷取的各類資訊，並說明在處理這些資訊時常見的錯誤類型與應避免的做法。\n"
"本單元將涵蓋以下四大主題：分類學資訊（taxonomic information）、空間資訊（spatial information）、採集資訊（collection information）、描述性資訊（descriptive information）。\n"
"請注意：每一筆出現紀錄（也就是你在資料庫或試算表中的每一列），都應包含上述四類資訊的對應欄位，這樣資料才能被妥善地分享與重複使用。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:38
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 7 \n"
msgstr "// Slide 7 \n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "Taxonomic information is an essential part of the data capture process.\n"
"Without it, a digitized specimen is useless and cannot be properly interpreted or reused.\n"
"Note that the species name is not the only type of taxonomic information that can be exploited in the data capture process: sometimes the specimen hasn’t been identified to the species, and higher taxonomic levels such as the genus or family are still useful for data managers and users.\n"
msgstr "分類學資訊（Taxonomic information）是資料擷取流程中不可或缺的一部分。\n"
"若缺乏分類學資訊，數位化後的標本將無法被正確詮釋或再利用，等同於失去其科學價值。\n"
"需要注意的是，「物種名稱」並不是資料擷取過程中唯一可用的分類資訊來源。有時標本尚未鑑定至物種階層，此時提供較高階的分類單元（如屬或科）依然對資料管理者與使用者具有實質參考價值。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:44 scripts/data-management.en.txt:63
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:43 scripts/definitions.en.txt:58
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:86 scripts/resourcing.en.txt:66
#: scripts/software.en.txt:50 scripts/structures.en.txt:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 8\n"
msgstr "// Slide 8\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "Most of the time, the scientific name is the main way of retrieving data in a database, portal, website, browser… Any error in the spelling or authority can lead to wrong or null queries, thus impeding the management and potential reuse of the data.\n"
"This is why it’s very important to check all categories of scientific names to fix errors and/or omissions.\n"
msgstr "在大多數情況下，「學名（scientific name）」是使用者查詢資料庫、入口網站、網頁或瀏覽器的主要依據。\n"
"因此，若在學名拼寫或權威命名（authority）上出現錯誤，都可能導致查無資料或查詢錯誤，進而影響資料的管理與後續使用。\n"
"這也是為什麼我們必須全面檢查所有類別的學名欄位，以便修正錯誤與補齊遺漏資訊。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:49 scripts/data-management.en.txt:73
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:47 scripts/definitions.en.txt:72
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:91 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:120
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:74 scripts/software.en.txt:59
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:132 scripts/structures.en.txt:54
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 9\n"
msgstr "// Slide 9\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:54
#, no-wrap
msgid "The most common issues occurring with taxon information are missing or inconsistent information, incorrect or non-atomic values, duplicates and uncertainty.\n"
"Always check the definitions and examples of taxonomic Darwin Core terms to avoid nomenclatural mistakes: http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/index.htm\n"
" \n"
"// Slide 10\n"
msgstr "最常見的分類學資訊問題包括：資料缺漏或不一致、錯誤或非原子化的欄位值、重複資料和不確定性標註。\n"
"為了避免命名錯誤，請務必查閱 Darwin Core 分類學欄位的定義與範例，以確保填寫正確無誤：http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/index.htm\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:60
#, no-wrap
msgid "Geographic information proves to be valuable in a lot of data re-use contexts, such as niche modelling or studies about species distribution.\n"
"While ‘old’ collections or specimens can be understandably difficult, if not impossible, to geolocate precisely, it is recommended to share precise coordinates or textual information when possible.\n"
"Coordinates should be recorded directly on the field when possible, along with the uncertainty and the geodetic datum used.\n"
"Otherwise use relevant and verified sources to geolocate your data.\n"
"You should note that coordinates or other geographic information can be generalized or not even shared at all in some contexts, such as with the conservation of sensitive species.\n"
msgstr "地理資訊在許多資料再利用的情境中都極具價值，例如生態棲位模型或物種分布研究。\n"
"雖然「舊有」典藏或標本往往難以、甚至無法精確定位其地理位置，但在可行的情況下，仍建議提供精確的座標或文字描述。\n\n"
"若有現場採集的機會，應於野外直接記錄座標，並同時註明位置不確定性與所使用的大地基準。\n"
"若無法直接記錄，則應使用可信且經驗證的資料來源來進行地理定位。\n\n"
"需要注意的是，在某些情境下，例如保育敏感物種，座標或其他地理資訊可能必須模糊化處理，甚至不予公開，以避免對物種造成潛在風險。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:62 scripts/data-management.en.txt:97
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:109 scripts/data-types.en.txt:57
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:88 scripts/documentation.en.txt:137
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:101 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:142
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:100 scripts/software.en.txt:68
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:178 scripts/structures.en.txt:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 11\n"
msgstr "// Slide 11\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:65
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spatial information can be found in numerous formats, not only geographic coordinates: examples include (but are not limited to) grid data, point+radius, or polygons.\n"
"Each of them is useful to share in order to check the consistency of the geographical elements (eg coordinates vs country code, or to ensure a given locality is consistent with a collector’s travels)\n"
msgstr "空間資訊（spatial information）可以存在於多種格式中，不僅限於地理座標（geographic coordinates）；舉例來說，包括但不限於網格資料（grid data）、點加半徑（point+radius）、或多邊形（polygons）等。\n"
"分享這些資訊有助於檢查地理元素的一致性，例如：座標與國家代碼（country code）是否相符，或是確認某一地點是否符合採集者的旅行路徑。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:67 scripts/data-management.en.txt:124
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:62 scripts/definitions.en.txt:106
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:105 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:154
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:109 scripts/structures.en.txt:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 12\n"
msgstr "// Slide 12\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:70
#, no-wrap
msgid "Within GBIF, it is recommended to share the geodetic Datum that was used to derive the coordinates (decimal latitude and longitude) shared.\n"
"In the absence of a specific geodetic datum, GBIF will infer WGS84 as default.\n"
msgstr "在 GBIF 的資料分享建議中，應一併提供產生座標（小數緯度與經度，decimal latitude and longitude）時所使用的地理基準（geodetic datum）。\n"
"若未特別標註地理基準，GBIF 將預設推論為 WGS84。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:72 scripts/data-types.en.txt:69
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:124 scripts/organizing.en.txt:109
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:162 scripts/resourcing.en.txt:119
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:205 scripts/structures.en.txt:84
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 13\n"
msgstr "// Slide 13\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "This slide shows an old GBIF map with different types of geographical issues: the most obvious one is a mirror effect between the USA and China (reversed coordinates), and you can also notice an artificial line along the Greenwich meridian where ‘0’ values were put in the ‘decimalLongitude’ field, as well as another one on the Equator where ‘0’ values were put in the ‘decimalLatitude’ field.\n"
"GBIF indexing now includes automatic geographical checks between the coordinates and countryCode shared within the dataset.\n"
"Coordinates can be automatically reversed to match the country. \n"
msgstr "這張投影片展示了一張舊版 GBIF 地圖，其中標示了不同類型的地理錯誤。最明顯的是美國與中國之間出現鏡像效應（座標反轉）；你也可以注意到格林威治子午線（Greenwich meridian）上出現了一條人為線條，這是因為在「decimalLongitude」欄位中出現了「0」值；赤道（Equator）上也出現另一條線，因為「decimalLatitude」欄位中出現了「0」值。\n"
"目前 GBIF 的索引系統已內建自動地理檢查功能，會比對資料集內分享的座標與國家代碼（countryCode），並可自動反轉座標以符合所屬國家。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:78 scripts/data-types.en.txt:74
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:128 scripts/organizing.en.txt:127
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:129 scripts/standards.en.txt:213
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:89
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 14\n"
msgstr "// Slide 14\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:81
#, no-wrap
msgid "Information about the context of the data collection or observation are very useful to share in order to give as much detail as possible regarding each occurrence.\n"
"Information such as the collector name, collection or observation protocol, habitat and other factors can prove to be important when reusing data for example with ecological niche modelling.\n"
msgstr "關於資料採集或觀察背景的資訊，也非常值得分享，以提供每筆出現紀錄更多細節。\n"
"例如：採集者姓名（collector name）、採集或觀察方法（collection or observation protocol）、棲地（habitat）及其他相關因子等，這些資訊在後續資料重用，例如：生態棲位建模（ecological niche modelling）時，可能會成為非常重要的參考依據。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:86
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 15\n"
"Data quality factors regarding collection information are mainly along the lines of exactitude like the correct collector name, consistency for example using the same vocabulary for describing soils, habitats, and completeness as in providing all existing information about the description of a given species including the  flowering period, colour of the leaves, and medicinal uses.\n"
"Within the Darwin Core and within the IPT you can find recommended controlled vocabularies for some fields such as the ‘lifeStage’.\n"
"The TDWG vocabularies task group works to promote and make better the ease of use across vocabularies.\n"
msgstr "投影片15\n"
"收集資訊的資料品質要素主要包括準確性（如採集者姓名是否正確）、一致性（如在描述土壤與棲地時使用相同的詞彙）、以及完整性（如提供關於特定物種描述的所有現有資訊，包括花期、葉片顏色與藥用用途）。\n"
"在達爾文核心標準與 IPT 中，可以找到針對部分欄位（例如：生命階段）所建議的控制詞彙。\n"
"TDWG 詞彙工作小組致力於推廣並改進這些詞彙表的可用性，以促進跨詞彙系統的一致與使用。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:88 scripts/data-types.en.txt:83
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:156 scripts/organizing.en.txt:147
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:161 scripts/standards.en.txt:245
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:100
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 16\n"
msgstr "// Slide 16\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keep in mind that descriptive information are often incomplete due to a whole array of factors.\n"
msgstr "請記住，描述性資訊往往因各種因素而不完整。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "Depending on the collection state, some labels can be incomplete or lacking crucial information; \n"
msgstr "根據標本或資料的保存狀況，有些標籤可能不完整，或缺少關鍵資訊\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "Completeness (for example of a species description) is often impossible to achieve with a single individual; \n"
msgstr "完整性（例如物種描述）往往無法僅依靠單一個體實現\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "and you should always check for consistency in your database or spreadsheet, for example in the terms used for describing colours, in order to avoid redundant information.\n"
msgstr "此外，應隨時檢查資料庫或試算表中的一致性，例如在顏色描述所使用的詞彙上，以避免出現重複或多餘的資訊。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:98 scripts/data-types.en.txt:88
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:162 scripts/organizing.en.txt:152
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:173 scripts/standards.en.txt:250
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:104
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 17\n"
msgstr "// Slide 17\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation has focused on the topic of data quality applied to data capture; indeed, these are the steps where it is crucial to ensure that all information related to each record is correctly and completely captured, in order for the data to be as clear and understandable as possible for future users.\n"
"This can only be done if consistent decisions are made at the institutional level in order to create a solid workflow for data capture and data management.\n"
"The chain of responsibility regarding data quality is then split between the persons involved at each step of the process, but keep in mind that data can always be improved and fixed if errors or omissions are detected at later stages.\n"
msgstr "本次演講重點探討了資料收集過程中的資料品質問題；事實上，在這些步驟中，確保與每筆記錄相關的所有資訊都得到正確、完整的採集至關重要，這樣才能確保資料對未來的使用者盡可能清晰易懂。\n\n"
"只有在機構層級做出一致的決策，以創建可靠的資料收集和資料管理工作流程，才能做到這一點。\n\n"
"資料品質的責任鏈隨後被分配給流程每個步驟的相關人員，但請記住，如果在後期階段檢測到錯誤或遺漏，資料總是可以改進和修復的。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:104 scripts/data-types.en.txt:92
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:174 scripts/organizing.en.txt:164
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:183 scripts/structures.en.txt:111
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 18\n"
msgstr "// Slide 18\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:106 scripts/data-management.en.txt:126
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:224 scripts/data-types.en.txt:137
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:212 scripts/documentation.en.txt:176
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:194 scripts/standards.en.txt:291
#, no-wrap
msgid "If you have questions on this presentation, please use the provided forum in the e-Learning platform.\n"
msgstr "如果對演講有任何疑問，請透過電子學習平台提供的討論區提出。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:108 scripts/data-types.en.txt:139
#, no-wrap
msgid "This video is part of a series of presentations used in the GBIF Biodiversity Data Mobilization course.\n"
msgstr "本影片屬於 GBIF 生物多樣性資料流通課程中一系列簡報的一部分。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:110 scripts/data-types.en.txt:141
#, no-wrap
msgid "The biodiversity data mobilization curriculum was originally developed as part of the Biodiversity Information Development Programme funded by the European Union.\n"
msgstr "生物多樣性資料流通課程最初是作為歐盟資助的生物多樣性資訊發展計劃的一部分而開發的。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-capture.en.txt:111
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation was originally created and narrated by Sophie Pamerlon with additional contributions by Laura Russell, BID and BIFA Trainers, Mentors and Students.\n"
msgstr "本簡報最初由 Sophie Pamerlon 製作並講述，並由 Laura Russell 以及 BID 與 BIFA 的講師、導師和學生提供額外貢獻。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this presentation, we are going to explore the key principles and processes important to effective data management.\n"
msgstr "在本次簡報中，我們將探討有效資料管理的重要原則和流程。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "The concept of data management includes a wide range of topics, from metadata formats to database organization.\n"
"In this presentation we are going to discuss an important set of principles necessary to improve data through the processes of data cleaning.\n"
msgstr "資料管理的概念涵蓋了從詮釋資料格式到資料庫組織等廣泛的主題。\n"
"在此簡報中，我們將討論透過資料清理流程來改善資料所必需的一組重要原則。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:13
#, no-wrap
msgid "What is data cleaning?\n"
msgstr "什麼是資料清理？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data Cleaning was defined by Arthur Chapman in 2005 as:\n"
msgstr "資料清理在 2005 年由 Arthur Chapman 定義為：\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "“A process used to determine inaccurate, incomplete, or unreasonable data and then improving the quality through correction of detected errors and omissions.”\n"
msgstr "「一種用於辨別不正確、不完整或不合理資料的過程，並透過修正所發現的錯誤與遺漏來提升資料品質」。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "The process of cleaning might include checks on formatting, completeness and reasonableness, the identification of outliers, review by content experts, and validation against accepted standards, rules, and conventions.\n"
msgstr "資料清理的過程可能包括格式檢查、完整性與合理性檢驗、離群值辨別、內容專家審查，以及依據公認標準、規則與慣例的驗證。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:21 scripts/data-types.en.txt:17
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:26 scripts/documentation.en.txt:42
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:33 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:28
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:22 scripts/software.en.txt:18
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 4\n"
msgstr "// Slide 4\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:23
#, no-wrap
msgid "Although there are many methods that can be applied while cleaning data, we view the process of cleaning in five general steps:\n"
msgstr "雖然在資料清理過程中可應用多種方法，但我們將資料清理流程概括為五個主要步驟：\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:25
#, no-wrap
msgid "First, we seek to determine and define the types of errors that are most likely present in our data.\n"
msgstr "首先，我們需要確定和定義我們的資料中最有可能存在的錯誤類型。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:27
#, no-wrap
msgid "Next, we conduct a search to identify the instances where those error have occurred.\n"
msgstr "接下來，我們進行搜尋以找出這些錯誤實際發生的情形。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "Then, we will correct those errors, whenever possible.\n"
msgstr "之後，我們會在可行的情況下修正這些錯誤。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "When we decide on the best means to make corrections, we want to document both the types of errors we find, as well as the solutions we have applied to them.\n"
msgstr "當我們決定最佳的修正方法時，我們希望記錄我們發現的錯誤類型以及我們對它們應用的解決方案。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we want to modify how data entry is practiced to reduce future errors of the same type. (Maletic & Marcus, 2000)\n"
msgstr "最後，我們希望改進資料輸入的作業方式，以減少未來出現相同類型錯誤的機會。 （Maletic & Marcus，2000）\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:37
#, no-wrap
msgid "One thing is certain: Errors are common and we can always expect to find them in the data we maintain.\n"
msgstr "有一點是確定的：錯誤很常見，我們總是會在我們維護的資料中發現它們。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "Our goal is to apply the best practices, including principles, processes, and tools, to make the data as fit for use as possible, even if we don’t know how data users will use the data.\n"
msgstr "我們的目標是應用最佳實踐，包括原則、流程和工具，使資料盡可能適合使用，即使我們不知道資料使用者將如何使用資料。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:45
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s take a look at several of the recommended principles of data cleaning.\n"
"These principles are presented for your consideration so that you can develop and improve upon your own data quality workflow.\n"
"The workflow that you create should represent the most efficient and effective process to achieve the highest data quality given your institution’s resources and expertise.\n"
msgstr "讓我們來看看幾項推薦的資料清理原則。\n"
"這些原則旨在提供參考，協助你建立並持續改進自己的資料品質工作流程。\n"
"你所設計的流程，應在機構可用的資源與專業能力範圍內，達成最高效且最有效的資料品質管理。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "Talk to your colleagues to learn about their workflows, but ultimately you should strive to create a workflow that will help you to achieve your data quality goals.\n"
msgstr "與同事交流以了解他們的工作流程，但最終仍應致力於建立一套能協助你達成資料品質目標的流程。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 7: Plan and Organize\n"
msgstr "投影片7：規劃與組織\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:51
#, no-wrap
msgid "The first two principles in data cleaning are planning and organization.\n"
msgstr "資料清理的前兩項原則是規劃與組織。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plan how you want the cleaning process to go.\n"
"A scattered or random approach is not likely to produce consistent results.\n"
"Develop and implement a strategy that fits with your institution’s resources and expertise.\n"
"A solid plan will improve both your data and the reputation of your institution among data users.\n"
msgstr "先規劃好資料清理的進行方式。\n"
"零散或隨意的做法難以產生一致的結果。\n"
"制定並實施符合你的機構資源和專業知識的策略。\n"
"完善的計畫不僅能提升資料品質，亦能增進機構在資料使用者之間的聲譽。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "One of the first steps of your plan should be to organize the data.\n"
"For example, you could organize your data by taxon and then send the appropriate subset of data to individuals with the knowledge or resources to clean based on names.\n"
"This can be done by geography or basis of record, or even in specifically sized packages of data in numeric order.\n"
"Work to the strengths of your institution.\n"
msgstr "你的計劃的第一步應該是整理資料。\n"
"例如，你可以按分類群整理數據，然後將相應的資料子集分配給具備相關知識或資源的人員，依物種名稱進行清理。\n"
"資料也可以依地理位置或紀錄類型來整理，甚至可以按數字順序將資料分成特定大小的資料夾。\n"
"應充分發揮機構的優勢來安排資料處理。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "As Arthur Chapman says “Prevention is better than cure.” \n"
"At the end of the day is always easier and less expensive to prevent an error than it is seek one out and correct it.\n"
"The more planning you do in advance, even all the way back to the moment that data is recorded in the field, the fewer errors you are likely to find.\n"
"Standardized vocabularies and clear procedures for data capture and data entry are only a few of the many tools you can use to prevent possible errors.\n"
msgstr "正如 Arthur Chapman 所說：「預防勝於治療。」\n"
"歸根究底，預防錯誤總比事後尋找並修正錯誤更簡單且成本更低。\n"
"越早進行規劃，甚至從資料在野外記錄的那一刻起就開始規劃，你發現錯誤的機率就越低。\n"
"使用標準化詞彙以及清晰的資料收集與輸入流程，僅是你可以用來預防潛在錯誤的眾多工具中的一部分。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:71
#, no-wrap
msgid "Be sure that everyone in your organization understands that data quality is everyone’s responsibility.\n"
"Of course, the primary responsibility lies with the people who maintain the data, but everyone, from field technicians to students performing data entry to emeritus curators have a responsibility to treat the data with care and to communicate any errors or inconsistencies they might discover.\n"
msgstr "請確保機構中的每個人都了解：資料品質是所有人的共同責任。\n"
"當然，主要責任在於負責維護資料的人員，但從野外技術人員、執行資料輸入的學生，到資深研究館員，每個人都有責任謹慎對待資料，並告知他們發現的任何錯誤或不一致之處。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:77
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data maintenance is only as effective as the expertise that support the process and no single individual can be expected to know everything about everything.\n"
"Developing partnerships with data users and content experts can help to make the data quality process more successful.\n"
"Reach out to your user community, both inside and outside of your institution, and work with them to help maintain your data at the highest quality.\n"
msgstr "資料維護的成效取決於支援該流程的專業知識，\n"
"而沒有人能夠對所有領域都瞭若指掌。\n"
"與資料使用者及領域專家建立合作關係，有助於提升資料品質管理的成效。\n"
"主動與機構內外的使用者社群聯繫，並與他們協作，共同維持資料的最高品質。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "Prioritize your cleaning to take advantage of the resources and knowledge that your institution possesses.\n"
"For example, you may choose to review all of the data recorded by collectors who are still alive so that important data is not lost later.\n"
"If you maintain a large dataset you might choose to prioritize data that can be cleaned at the lowest cost using automated processes and then prioritize the more difficult errors that require individual attention.\n"
"Ultimately, you should prioritize the data that is of the greatest value to the work of your institution and then move through the data in a logical and orderly manner.\n"
msgstr "在進行資料清理時，應根據機構現有的資源與專業知識來設定優先順序。\n"
"例如，你可以優先檢視由仍在世的採集者所記錄的資料，以避免重要資訊在日後遺失。\n"
"若你管理的是大型資料集，則可以先處理能以自動化方式、低成本完成清理的部分，再將重點放在那些需要人工判斷與處理的複雜錯誤上。\n"
"最後，你應優先處理對機構業務最具價值的資料，並以有條理、循序漸進的方式推進整體清理流程。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:84 scripts/data-types.en.txt:52
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:84 scripts/documentation.en.txt:130
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:96 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:129
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:84 scripts/software.en.txt:64
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 10\n"
msgstr "// Slide 10\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some times it can be an effective strategy to set some measures of performance before the cleaning process begins.\n"
"One performance measure might be to complete the cleaning of 500 records every week.\n"
"Another measure might be to provide a statistical report of the accuracy of the corrections being made, such as:\n"
msgstr "有時，在資料清理開始前設定績效指標，會是一項有效的策略。\n"
"例如，可以設定每週完成 500 筆資料清理作業作為績效目標。\n"
"另一種衡量方式則可透過統計報告呈現修正結果的準確度，例如：\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "“95% of all georeferences corrected this week now have an uncertainty in meters of less than 1000 meters.”\n"
msgstr "「本週修正的地理參考資料中，有 95% 的不確定性已縮小至 1000 公尺以內。」\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "These kinds of measures can give direction and guidance to the people performing the cleaning, as well as, to serve as a means to report on successes and areas in need of improvement.\n"
msgstr "這樣的指標能為執行資料清理的人員提供方向與指引，\n"
"同時也可作為回報成果與辨別需改進之處的依據。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:95
#, no-wrap
msgid "In an ideal world, you will have developed a data maintenance plan that has been optimized to be as efficient and effective as possible.\n"
"Optimization can be achieved in many different ways including taking advantage of your institution’s strengths, setting clear objectives and measure of success, and regular adjustment of your priorities based on available resources.\n"
msgstr "在理想的情況下，你應該已建立一套經過最佳化、兼具效率與有效性的資料維護計畫。\n"
"最佳化可以透過多種方式達成，包括善用機構的優勢、設定明確的目標與成功衡量標準，並根據可用資源定期調整優先順序。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:103
#, no-wrap
msgid "One of the best ways to learn how successful your data cleaning and data maintenance processes are, is to seek feedback from the data users in your community.\n"
"This can be accomplished through the partnerships we discussed earlier, but you can also seek feedback in other ways.\n"
"Once such way is to review the way that GBIF presents your data.\n"
"You can view the comparison between the verbatim data published to GBIF and the interpreted data that GBIF might have corrected or updated.\n"
"These comparisons can help you to optimize your data cleaning process, but you can also help GBIF to improve its data quality processes, too, when you provide expert feedback back to them.\n"
msgstr "評估資料清理與資料維護流程成效的最佳方式之一，是向社群中的資料使用者尋求回饋。\n"
"這可以透過前面提到的合作關係來進行，也能透過其他方式取得意見。\n"
"其中一種方法是檢視 GBIF 平台上你的資料呈現方式。\n"
"你可以比較自己發佈至 GBIF 的原始資料與 GBIF 經過解讀、修正或更新後的資料。\n"
"這些比較能協助你優化資料清理流程，同時當你將專業意見回饋給 GBIF 時，也能幫助 GBIF 改進整體資料品質流程。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "One of the best ways to achieve success with data cleaning is to provide training and education to everyone who works with your data.\n"
"These may be trainings that you organize yourself, local or regional trainings in partnership with, or presented by, other institutions in your area, or workshops and courses provided by other groups, such as GBIF.\n"
"If you need training, please check the GBIF web site regularly or contact GBIF’s Community Mentors or GBIF’s Biodiversity Open Data Ambassadors who also can be good resources for training and education opportunities.\n"
msgstr "在資料清理工作中取得成功的最佳方法之一，是為所有處理資料的人員提供培訓與教育。\n"
"這些培訓可以由你自行規劃，也可以與當地或區域內的其他機構合作舉辦，或參加由其他組織如 GBIF所提供的工作坊與課程。\n"
"如果你有培訓需求，請定期瀏覽 GBIF 官方網站，或聯繫 GBIF 社群導師或生物多樣性開放資料推廣大使，他們也可以為你提供培訓和教育訓練的管道。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:112
#, no-wrap
msgid "Documentation is one of the most important principles of data cleaning.\n"
"This is true in two key ways.\n"
msgstr "文件記錄是資料清理中最重要的原則之一。\n"
"這一點在兩個方面尤為重要。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:117
#, no-wrap
msgid "First, whatever processes you use to maintain your data, it is important to be transparent about how errors are discovered and corrected.\n"
"This transparency can be truly successful when the data cleaning process is well documented.\n"
"Without clear and accessible documentation, you run the risk of losing best practices, building redundant processes and losing optimization.\n"
"Good documentation will help you to avoid recurring errors.\n"
msgstr "首先，無論你採用何種方式維護資料，都應對錯誤的發現與修正過程保持透明。\n"
"當資料清理流程有完整記錄時，這種透明度才能真正發揮效果。\n"
"若缺乏清楚且可取得的文件記錄，你可能會面臨失去最佳實踐、建立重複的流程，以及失去最佳化的風險。\n"
"良好的文件記錄能幫助你避免錯誤重複發生。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:122
#, no-wrap
msgid "Documentation is also key to good data quality.\n"
"Good documentation, in metadata, for example, allows data users to determine the fitness for use of your data.\n"
"Your documentation should not only include good metadata, it should also include the best practices, standardized vocabularies, and taxonomic and geographic authorities used.\n"
"Documenting what data was cleaned by whom will help you to improve your optimization, reach performance measures, and ultimately, tell a more complete story of where your data came from and how it was improved over time.\n"
msgstr "文件記錄對維持良好的資料品質也很重要。\n"
"例如，完整的描述性資料可以讓資料使用者判斷資料的適用性。\n"
"你的文件記錄不僅應包含完整的描述性資料，還應記錄所使用的最佳實踐、標準化詞彙，以及所使用的分類和地理規範。\n"
"記錄每筆資料由誰清理過，能幫助你提升流程最佳化、達成績效指標，且最終能夠更完整地呈現資料的來源，以及它是如何隨時間被逐步改善的。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:129 scripts/data-quality.en.txt:227
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:179 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "This video is part of a series of presentations used in the GBIF Biodiversity Data Mobilization course.\n"
"The biodiversity data mobilization curriculum was originally developed as part of the Biodiversity Information Development Programme funded by the European Union.\n"
msgstr "本影片屬於 GBIF 生物多樣性資料流通課程的一系列簡報。\n"
"生物多樣性資料流通課程最初是作為歐洲聯盟資助的生物多樣性資訊發展計畫的一部分所開發。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-management.en.txt:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation was originally created by Nestor Beltran with additional contributions by David Bloom, BID and BIFA Trainers, Mentors and Students.\n"
"This presentation has been narrated by David Bloom.\n"
msgstr "本簡報最初由 Nestor Beltran 製作，並由 David Bloom、BID 與 BIFA 的講師、導師及學員共同完善。\n"
"本簡報由 David Bloom 講述。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 1\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 1\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this presentation, we are going to introduce some of the concepts that are important to understand basic data quality.\n"
msgstr "在本簡報中，我們將介紹一些對於理解基本資料品質很重要的概念。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 2\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 2\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "The two primary concepts that we will explore are Fitness for Use and Measures of Quality.\n"
"These two ideas will lay the foundation for more detailed data quality concepts and techniques in the future.\n"
msgstr "我們將探討的兩個主要概念是適用性與品質衡量。\n"
"這兩個概念將為之後更深入的資料品質理論與技術奠定基礎。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 3\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 3\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "Before we begin, I’d like to take a moment to explain why data quality is important.\n"
"Cleanliness is next to Godliness is an expression that we sometimes use to describe the importance of data quality.\n"
"In this case, Godliness can be understood as holiness, and when something is holy it is considered by many to be worthy of our trust.\n"
"So, in the case of data quality, we are more likely to trust and to use data that has been cleaned and cared for.\n"
msgstr "在開始之前，我想先說明一下為什麼資料品質這麼重要。\n"
"我們有時會用一句話來形容它的重要性：「整潔近乎虔誠」。\n"
"這裡的虔誠可以理解為神聖，而被視為神聖的事物，通常也更值得我們信任。\n"
"同樣地，當資料經過清理與維護，我們更可能去信任並使用這些資料。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 4\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 4\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "Stepping back into the domain of biodiversity informatics, the classic definition of data quality was coined in 2005 by Arthur Chapman.\n"
msgstr "回到生物多樣性資訊學的領域，資料品質的經典定義是由 Arthur Chapman 在2005年提出的。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "He wrote: “Data quality is related to use and cannot be assessed independently of the user.\n"
"In a database, the data have no actual quality or value (Dalcin 2004); they only have potential value that is realized when someone uses the data to do something useful.\n"
"Information quality relates to its ability to satisfy its customers and to meet customers’ needs (English 1999).”\n"
msgstr "他提到：「資料品質取決於用途，不能脫離使用者來評估。\n"
"在資料庫裡，資料本身沒有真正的品質或價值（Dalcin 2004）；只有當有人利用這些資料做出有意義的事時，它們的潛在價值才會被發揮。而資訊的品質，則在於它能否滿足使用者的需求與期待（English 1999）。」\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:27
#, no-wrap
msgid "And so, for us, as individuals who are responsible for maintaining data, we want the data we publish to be as satisfying to all of our potential users as possible.\n"
"To do this, we seek to provide the highest quality data.\n"
msgstr "對於我們這些負責維護資料的人而言，我們期望所發布的資料能盡可能滿足所有潛在使用者的需求。\n"
"為了達成此目標，我們致力於提供最高品質的資料。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 5\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 5\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:32
#, no-wrap
msgid "To help you to accomplish this task, we will explore Fitness for Use.\n"
"We will also explore the Measures of Quality, specifically, Correctness and Consistency.\n"
msgstr "為協助你達成此目標，我們將探討適用性。\n"
"同時，我們也將介紹品質衡量，特別是正確性與一致性。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:34
#, no-wrap
msgid "These are important concepts that you will need to know and be able to articulate about your own data or data that you maintain.\n"
msgstr "這些都是你在管理或維護資料時必須理解並能清楚闡述的重要概念。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 6\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 6\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s begin with an example of Fitness for Use.\n"
"A shoemaker creates a pair of clogs for the purpose of covering a person’s feet, just like those at the top of this image.\n"
msgstr "我們先從適用性的例子開始說明。\n"
"鞋匠製作一雙木屐，用來覆蓋並保護人的雙腳，就如同圖中上方所示的木屐。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 7\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 7\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:43
#, no-wrap
msgid "When the shoemaker made these shoes, did he know that this girl would use them for dancing?\n"
msgstr "當鞋匠製作這雙鞋時，他是否知道這個女孩會用它來跳舞呢？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:45
#, no-wrap
msgid "Maybe.\n"
msgstr "或許吧。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 8\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 8\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "Do you think that the same shoemaker knew that a gardener might one day use the shoes as plant pots?\n"
msgstr "你認為同一位鞋匠是否知道，有一天園丁可能會將這雙鞋當作花盆使用呢？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:53
#, no-wrap
msgid "Maybe not.\n"
" \n"
"// SLIDE 9\n"
msgstr "或許不知道。\n"
"// 投影片9\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "We often hear people talk about data’s Fitness for Use in the ecological sciences, but what we need to remember is that data is not inherently good or bad.\n"
"Rather it is the user of the data who gives data its value.\n"
msgstr "在生態科學領域，我們經常聽到人們談論資料的適用性，\n"
"但我們需要記住，資料本身並無固有的好壞之分。\n"
"資料的價值，是由使用資料的人所賦予的。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:59
#, no-wrap
msgid "For example, for one person, data identified to the level of Genus may be sufficient to run predictive models of ecological niches.\n"
"For a person studying a particular taxon, that same genus-level data will be much less useful than more detailed occurrences with subspecies information.\n"
msgstr "例如，對某些人而言，分類至屬層級的資料已足以用來建立生態棲位的預測模型。然而，對研究特定分類群的人來說，同樣屬層級的資料，其價值遠低於包含亞種資訊的更詳細出現記錄。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 10\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 10\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:63
#, no-wrap
msgid "So, what is Fitness for Use and how does it relate to data?\n"
msgstr "所以，什麼是適用性？它與資料有何關聯呢？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:65
#, no-wrap
msgid "When Chapman discusses Fitness for Use, he says that once a dataset has been created and shared there are two primary perspectives on how those data might be used; the perspective of the creator and the perspective of the user.\n"
msgstr "當 Chapman 討論適用性時，他指出，一旦資料集被建立並分享，就會出現兩種主要的資料使用視角：創建者的視角與使用者的視角。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "To help somebody to decide if your data is trustworthy or useful enough for them to use, you have to understand your data and how to convey those data to the potential user.\n"
"These are some of the important questions about the metadata, or characteristics of your data, that you should be able to answer and share with others:\n"
msgstr "為了讓他人判斷你的資料是否可靠或足夠有用，你必須了解自己的資料，並掌握如何將這些資訊傳達給潛在使用者。\n"
"以下是一些關於描述性資料或資料特性的重要問題，你應該能夠回答並與他人分享：\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:72
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Accessible are your data? \n"
"How easily can someone access your data? \n"
"People can’t use the data if they can’t find the data.\n"
msgstr "你的資料有多容易取得？\n"
"其他人能多方便地存取你的資料？\n"
"如果找不到資料，人們就無法使用它。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Accurate are your data? \n"
"Can your data be trusted? \n"
"For example, are your identifications current and were they made by known experts?\n"
msgstr "你的資料有多準確？\n"
"你的資料能被信賴嗎？\n"
"例如，資料中的物種鑑定是否為最新，且是否由公認的專家完成？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Timely are they? \n"
"When will the data be made available? \n"
"How often are they updated? \n"
msgstr "你的資料更新的時效性如何？\n"
"資料何時可供使用？\n"
"更新頻率為何？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:84
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Complete or Comprehensive are the data? \n"
"Which parts of your dataset are documented fully? \n"
"How well do the data cover a particular time, place, or domain?\n"
msgstr "你的資料有多完整或詳盡？\n"
"資料集中的哪些項目有完整記錄？\n"
"資料對特定的時間、地點或研究範疇的涵蓋範圍如何？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Consistent are they? \n"
"Is the data in each field always of the same type? \n"
"Was the data collected using the same documented protocols?\n"
msgstr "你的資料有多一致？\n"
"每個欄位中的資料是否始終保持相同的型態？\n"
"資料是否依照相同的、已記錄的收集規範進行收集？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:91
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Relevant are they? \n"
"How similar is this dataset to others that have been used successfully for the same purpose?\n"
msgstr "你的資料有多相關？\n"
"這個資料集與其他已成功用於相同目的的資料集相比，有多相似？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:95
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Detailed are the data? \n"
"How much resolution is there in your data? \n"
"At what scale can they be used for mapping?\n"
msgstr "你的資料有多詳細？\n"
"資料的解析度如何？\n"
"可用於製作地圖的比例或尺度為何？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:99
#, no-wrap
msgid "Is the data Easy to interpret? \n"
"Is the dataset documented in a clear and concise way? \n"
"If your documents are handwritten, are they legible?\n"
msgstr "資料容易理解嗎？\n"
"資料集是否以清楚、簡明的方式記錄？\n"
"若文件為手寫形式，是否清晰可辨？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:101
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 11\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 11\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:103
#, no-wrap
msgid "While Fitness for Use is subjective, measures of data quality are much less so.\n"
msgstr "雖然適用性具有主觀性，但資料品質的衡量則相對客觀得多。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:106
#, no-wrap
msgid "Chapman states that: \"All data include error – there is no escaping it! \n"
"It is knowing what the error is that is important, and knowing if the error is within acceptable limits for the purpose to which the data are to be put.”\n"
msgstr "Chapman 指出：「所有資料都存在錯誤—這是無法避免的！\n"
"重要的是要了解錯誤的性質，並判斷該錯誤是否在資料預定用途可接受的範圍內。」\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:108
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 12\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 12\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:110
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can use two measures of quality, Correctness and Consistency, to help us to document these inherent errors in data.\n"
msgstr "我們可以透過兩個品質衡量指標—正確性與一致性，來協助記錄資料中固有的錯誤。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:112
#, no-wrap
msgid "Correctness, sometimes called accuracy, is “How close the recorded value is to the actual, real-world value”.\n"
msgstr "正確性有時也稱為準確性，是指「記錄的數值與真實世界中實際數值的接近程度」。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:114
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this diagram accuracy is how close a given dot is to the centre of the target.\n"
msgstr "在此圖中，準確性是指給定點與目標中心的距離。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:116
#, no-wrap
msgid "Consistency, sometimes called precision, is “How often you get it right”\n"
msgstr "一致性有時也稱為精確性，是指「你能多常得到正確結果的程度」\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this diagram, precision is how close the dots are together irrespective of how close they are to the centre of the target.\n"
msgstr "在此圖中，精確性是指點之間的接近程度，而不管它們與目標中心的接近程度。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:121
#, no-wrap
msgid "These are measures of how well the data gatherer was able to capture the true value being investigated.\n"
"Knowing these properties of your data will help you to understand the ways in which you can and cannot clean, validate and process the data.\n"
msgstr "這些指標反映了資料蒐集者在多大程度上能夠準確捕捉所研究的真實數值。\n"
"了解資料的這些特性，有助於判斷在資料清理、驗證與處理過程中，哪些方法可行、哪些則不可行。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:123 scripts/documentation.en.txt:174
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 13\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 13\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:126
#, no-wrap
msgid "This image provides a clear set of illustrations of accuracy and precision.\n"
"This may be a good image to keep with you when you have to explain these concepts to others.\n"
msgstr "這張圖清楚地說明了準確性與精確性的概念。\n"
"當你需要向他人解釋這兩個概念時，這會是一張很實用的參考圖。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:128
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 14\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 14\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s look at some examples of Correctness, or accuracy, and how it can help us think about the practicalities of data cleaning.\n"
"Remember that Correctness is how close you are to the center of a target.\n"
msgstr "讓我們來看一些關於正確性或準確性的例子，以及它如何幫助我們思考資料清理的實用性。\n"
"請記住，正確性是指你距離目標中心的距離。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s look at an example.\n"
msgstr "讓我們來看一個例子。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:135
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 15\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 15\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:138
#, no-wrap
msgid "For this example, let’s imagine your dataset contains records of fossil bird specimens from the Early Triassic Period.\n"
"The taxonomic name for a number of specimens in your dataset is recorded as Thismia.\n"
msgstr "在這個例子中，假設你的資料集中包含早三疊紀時期的化石鳥類標本紀錄。\n"
"其中有一些標本的分類學名稱被記錄為 Thismia。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:140
#, no-wrap
msgid "Do you know if Thismia is a taxon of ancient birds?\n"
msgstr "你知道 Thismia 是否屬於古代鳥類的分類單元嗎？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:143
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 16\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 16\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:145
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this case, the answer is no! \n"
msgstr "在這個例子中，答案是否定的\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:147
#, no-wrap
msgid "Thismia is a very rare plant from the State of Illinois in the United States.\n"
msgstr "Thismia 其實是一種非常罕見的植物，分布於美國伊利諾州。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:151
#, no-wrap
msgid "As a result, the correctness, or accuracy, of the taxonomic name is low.\n"
"Perhaps because the data entry technician was not a paleontologist.\n"
"As a result, all of the names in the dataset might need to be checked and corrected by an expert before the data could be used.\n"
msgstr "因此，這些分類學名稱的正確性也就是準確性很低。\n"
"可能的原因是資料輸入人員並非古生物學家。\n"
"因此，在資料可供使用之前，資料集中的所有名稱可能都需要由專家進行核對與修正。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:153
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 17\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 17\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s look at another example.\n"
"Here, your dataset contains specimens collected in a place called Kalamazoo by a scientist named Richard Spruce.\n"
msgstr "讓我們再看一個例子。\n"
"在這個例子中，你的資料集中包含由科學家 Richard Spruce 所採集，地點標記為 Kalamazoo 的標本。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:158
#, no-wrap
msgid "We must ask ourselves:\n"
msgstr "我們必須問我的自己：\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "Is 49007 the right postal code for Kalamazoo?\n"
msgstr "49007 是 Kalamazoo 的正確郵遞區號嗎？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:162
#, no-wrap
msgid "Did Richard Spruce collect in Michigan?\n"
msgstr "Richard Spruce 是在密西根州採集標本？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:164
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 18\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 18\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:166
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this case, the answer is yes to our first question, 49007 is a postal code for Kalamazoo, so the accuracy of the locality is good, but what about the collector, Richard Spruce?\n"
msgstr "在這個例子中，對於第一個問題的答案是對的：49007 的確是 Kalamazoo 的郵遞區號，因此地點的準確性良好。\n"
"但採集者 Richard Spruce 呢？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:168
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 19\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 19\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:172
#, no-wrap
msgid "There is, in fact, a famous botanist named Richard Spruce.\n"
"He was born in 1817 and died in 1893, but he conducted his collecting in the Amazon Basin and Andes mountains in South America.\n"
"Something is not quite right, so we need to find out more about the collector before we can be confident in the correctness of these data.\n"
msgstr "事實上，的確有一位著名植物學家名為 Richard Spruce。\n"
"他生於 1817 年，死於 1893 年，但他的採集活動主要在南美洲的亞馬遜盆地與安地斯山脈。\n"
"這裡出現了問題，因此在能對這些資料的正確性有信心之前，我們需要進一步確認採集者的身分。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:174
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 20\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 20\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:176
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s take a look now at what Consistency, or precision, means to data.\n"
msgstr "現在讓我們來看看一致性或稱精確性對資料的意義。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:178
#, no-wrap
msgid "Consistency represents how many times you get the same answer, regardless of where the center of the target lies.\n"
msgstr "一致性代表你能多次得到相同結果，而不考慮目標的中心的位置。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:182
#, no-wrap
msgid "For example, your dataset contains botanical occurrences including a field, or column, named “Full Name”.\n"
"This field contains the name of the specimen collector.\n"
"As you can see there are five different names, but how many unique collectors are there? Can you tell just by looking at the names?\n"
msgstr "例如，你的資料集中包含植物出現紀錄，其中有一個欄位名為「全名」。\n"
"這個欄位記錄標本的採集者姓名。\n"
"如你所見，欄位中出現了五種不同的名字，但實際上有多少位不同的採集者呢？只透過名字，你能判斷出來嗎？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:184
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 21\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 21\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:186
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sometimes, if you are familiar with the dataset, you might know how many collectors are represented just by looking at the data, but if you don’t know these data well, you might need to do some research, ask some questions or look at the original field notes.\n"
msgstr "有時，如果你對資料集非常熟悉，僅透過觀察資料就可能知道包含了多少位採集者；\n"
"但如果你不熟悉這些資料，則可能需要進行一些調查、提出問題，或參考原始的野外筆記。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:188
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this example, if you guessed that these five names represent two or three unique collectors, you would be right.\n"
msgstr "在這個例子中，如果你猜測這五個名字代表兩到三位不同的採集者，那麼你的判斷是正確的。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:191
#, no-wrap
msgid "In blue, Joseph Dalton Hooker AND Hook.f.\n"
"are the same person.\n"
msgstr "以藍色標示的 Joseph Dalton Hooker 與 Hook.f. 其實是同一人。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:193
#, no-wrap
msgid "Period is the abbreviation used for Joseph Dalton Hooker (1817-1911).\n"
msgstr "句號是 Joseph Dalton Hooker (1817-1911) 的縮寫。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "In green, W.J. Hooker and Hook. are the same person.\n"
"Period is the abbreviation for William Jackson Hooker (1785-1865).\n"
"William was actually Joseph’s father.\n"
msgstr "以綠色標示的 W.J. Hooker 與 Hook. 其實是同一人。\n"
"句號是 William Jackson Hooker（1785–1865）的縮寫。\n"
"William 其實是 Joseph 的父親。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:201
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, in the red, there is “Hooker, J.” \n"
"This entry could be an error or typo of either Joseph or William Hooker, OR it could be a third person with a similar name.\n"
"Thus, more research would be needed to clarify these data.\n"
msgstr "最後，紅色部分是「Hooker, J」。這個紀錄可能是 Joseph 或 William Hooker 的錯誤或拼字錯誤，也可能是另一位同名的第三人。\n"
"因此，需要進一步調查才能釐清這筆資料。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:203
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 22\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 22\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:206
#, no-wrap
msgid "We have just discussed several simple illustrations of two measures of data quality.\n"
"These examples should give you a basic understanding about how to identify the inaccuracies and imprecisions in data.\n"
msgstr "我們剛剛討論了兩個資料品質衡量指標的幾個簡單範例。\n"
"這些例子應能讓你對如何判斷資料中的不正確性與不一致性有基本的理解。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:208
#, no-wrap
msgid "How we document and fix these issues or errors is a process that we call data cleaning.\n"
msgstr "我們用來記錄並修正這些問題或錯誤的過程，被稱為資料清理。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:210
#, no-wrap
msgid "More specifically:\n"
msgstr "更具體地說：\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:213
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data cleaning is the process of correcting or removing dirty data caused by contradictions, disparities, keying mistakes, missing bits, and more.\n"
"It also includes validation of the changes made, and may require normalization.\n"
msgstr "資料清理是指對因矛盾、不一致、輸入錯誤、資料遺漏等原因造成的「髒資料」進行修正或刪除的過程。\n"
"這個過程同時包含所做更改的驗證，並且可能需要進行標準化。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:215
#, no-wrap
msgid "Our data cleaning, or lack thereof, will affect how data users perceive your data’s fitness for use.\n"
msgstr "我們的資料清理或缺乏資料清理將影響資料使用者如何看待你的資料是否適用。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:217
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is not necessary for you to do all the data cleaning yourself, in fact, a common misconception is that all errors MUST be fixed before you can share your data.\n"
msgstr "你不必親自完成所有資料清理；事實上，一個常見的誤解是，所有錯誤必須在分享資料之前全部修正。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:220
#, no-wrap
msgid "The truth is that you can only clean as much data as your time, knowledge, and resources permit.\n"
"As a result, it is essential to document what you know and what you do not know about your data so that when you do share them, data users will know the level of the quality of your data.\n"
msgstr "事實上，你只能在時間、知識和資源允許的範圍內清理盡可能多的資料。\n"
"因此，清楚記錄你對資料的了解與未知之處是很重要的，這樣在分享資料時，使用者才能掌握資料的品質水準。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:222
#, no-wrap
msgid "// SLIDE 23\n"
msgstr "// SLIDE 23\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-quality.en.txt:229
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation was originally created by Sharon Grant with additional contributions by Dag Endresen, David Bloom, BID and BIFA Trainers, Mentors and Students.\n"
"This presentation has been narrated by David Bloom.\n"
msgstr "本簡報最初由 Sharon Grant 創建，Dag Endresen、David Bloom、BID 和 BIFA 培訓師、導師和學生也參與了貢獻。\n"
"本簡報由 David Bloom 講述。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this presentation, we’ll have a look at the different types of data that can be called ‘primary biodiversity data’ and shared within GBIF.\n"
"These data can be complex and have different origins; we’ll see how they can be structured into one of the three data classes accepted by GBIF, based on the Darwin Core data sharing standard that is used within the GBIF community.\n"
msgstr "在本簡報中，我們將探討可稱為「原始生物多樣性資料」並在 GBIF 內部共享的不同類型的資料。\n"
"這些數據可能非常複雜且來源多樣；我們將了解如何依據 GBIF 社群採用的 Darwin Core 資料分享標準，將其結構化為 GBIF 所接受的三種資料類別之一。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "As you can see on this slide, GBIF currently supports four types of dataset: Resource datasets (e.g. metadata-only datasets), species checklists, Occurrence datasets and Sampling-event datasets.\n"
"I will not discuss Resource datasets in this presentation as metadata-only datasets are not supported by the GBIF-funded programmes.\n"
msgstr "如同投影片上所示，GBIF 目前支援四種類型的資料集：\n"
"資源資料集（例如僅含詮釋資料的資料集）、物種名錄、出現紀錄資料集，以及調查活動資料集。\n"
"在本簡報中，我們不會討論資源資料集，因為僅包含詮釋資料的資料集並不在 GBIF 資助計畫的支援範圍內。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "The first type of dataset that can be published within GBIF is the checklist, aka a list of species or higher taxa.\n"
"In a checklist, the main unit is the taxon, not the occurrence (individual).\n"
msgstr "在 GBIF 中可發布的第一種類型資料集是名錄，也就是物種或更高階分類群的清單。\n"
"在名錄中，主要的單位是分類群，而非（個體的）出現紀錄。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "Checklists, and more generally, taxonomical datasets can vary in scope : \n"
msgstr "名錄，更一般地說，分類資料集的範圍可能有很大的差異\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:21
#, no-wrap
msgid "a list of species present in a protected area is a checklist.\n"
msgstr "一份列出保護區內所有物種的清單，就是一個名錄。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:23
#, no-wrap
msgid "a national or thematic list of taxa (e.g. Butterflies of Laos, or the Flora of Malaysia), which sometimes provide more info on the distribution, taxonomical hierarchy and synonyms of the taxa is a checklist.\n"
msgstr "一份以國家或主題為範圍的分類群名錄（例如：寮國的蝴蝶－、或馬來西亞植物誌），有時還會提供分類群的分布資訊、分類階層與同物異名，這也是一種名錄。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:25
#, no-wrap
msgid "IUCN red lists (at the international, national or regional levels) also are checklists and can give more information about the vulnerability status of the listed taxa. \n"
msgstr "IUCN 瀕危物種紅色名錄（無論是國際、國家或區域層級）也是一種名錄，並可提供所列分類群的受脅或保育狀態等相關資訊\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "GBIF provides data publishers with templates for each class of dataset.\n"
"The GBIF template for checklists (taxon Data) allows you to share information linked to each species or taxa: its ID, full name and authorship, relationship to other taxa, geographical details and so on.\n"
"In this template, one line represents one taxon and a taxon can only appear once in the dataset (otherwise it will create a duplicate).\n"
msgstr "GBIF 為每一類型的資料集提供對應的模板。\n"
"針對名錄（分類群資料），GBIF 的模板可用來分享與每個物種或分類群相關的資訊，例如：其 ID、完整學名與命名者、與其他分類群的關係、地理資訊等。\n"
"在此模板中，一列代表一個分類群，而同一分類群在資料集中只能出現一次（否則會造成重複）。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "The second type of data that can be published within GBIF is data coming from natural history or scientific collections, also known as specimens.\n"
"Unlike checklists, the main unit in a specimen collection will be the specimen (a single organism or group of organisms), and you can have multiple specimens from the same species or taxon group.\n"
msgstr "第二類可在 GBIF 上發布的資料，來自自然史或科學典藏，也就是所謂的標本資料。\n"
"與名錄不同，標本資料的主要單位是標本（單一個體或一群個體），同一物種或分類群可能會有多個不同的標本。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:38 scripts/definitions.en.txt:44
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:81 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:105
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:51 scripts/software.en.txt:39
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:93 scripts/structures.en.txt:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 7\n"
msgstr "// Slide 7\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "Collections and specimens vastly differ in numbers, size and preservation methods.\n"
"However, any kind of collection that holds preserved living organisms can be captured and shared within GBIF; herbarium or zoological collections (including fossils) for example, but geology or human artifacts collections do not fall under the GBIF scope.\n"
msgstr "各類典藏與標本在數量、大小與保存方式上差異極大。\n"
"然而，只要是保存生物個體的典藏，都可以在 GBIF 平台上被記錄與分享，例如植物標本館藏或動物典藏（包含化石）。\n"
"但地質標本或人類文物等收藏，則不屬於 GBIF 的資料範疇。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:45
#, no-wrap
msgid "Biodiversity data can also be found in technical and scientific literature: it is possible to compile and share this kind of data within GBIF, but you should be extra-cautious not to share duplicate data (for example, a specimen described in a scientific article might already be present on GBIF.org in the dataset of its collection)\n"
msgstr "生物多樣性資料也常見於技術與科學文獻中：這類資料可以整理後在 GBIF 上分享，但需要特別注意不要分享重複資料。例如，一個在科學文章中描述的標本，可能已經存在於其典藏資料集的 GBIF.org 上\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:50
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the absence of digitized datasets, biodiversity data can be extracted and compiled from scientific articles, phD or master theses, reports and other documents.\n"
"ALWAYS contact the data owner first when compiling literature data to ask for permission to publish them to GBIF ! \n"
msgstr "在數位化資料集缺乏的情況下，生物多樣性資料可以從科學文章、博士或碩士論文、報告以及其他文件中擷取並整理。\n"
"在整理文獻資料時，務必先聯繫資料擁有者，徵得同意後再發布到 GBIF\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:55
#, no-wrap
msgid "A large majority of the data available on GBIF.org now comes from fieldwork records.\n"
"These data can be observed and/or collected using different protocols in the field, by scientists, naturalists or amateur citizen-scientists via programmes and apps.\n"
msgstr "目前 GBIF.org 上的大多數資料來自實地調查紀錄。\n"
"這些資料可透過不同的現地調查方法由科學家、博物學家或公民科學家利用各類計畫與應用程式進行觀察或採集。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:60
#, no-wrap
msgid "These field data can be compiled in survey reports, environmental impact assessment studies, field notes and other logs.\n"
"They can be linked to other types of data, such as checklists, which are complementary to occurrence data observed or collected in the wilderness.\n"
msgstr "這些實地調查資料可以整理成調查報告、環境影響評估研究、野外筆記及其他紀錄。\n"
"它們也可以與其他類型的資料相關聯，例如名錄，這些資料可作為在野外觀察或採集的出現紀錄的補充。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:67
#, no-wrap
msgid "Occurrence data represents the GBIF class of datasets with the largest number of records on GBIF.org.\n"
"Collection (aka specimens), literature and fieldwork data can be shared within GBIF using this template, which focuses on the observed individual or collected specimen.\n"
"In this kind of dataset, multiple individuals or specimens can be recorded for a single taxon, as long as they each have a unique identifier.\n"
"Other fields for occurrence data include where, when, how and by whom was each occurrence observed and/or collected in the field.\n"
msgstr "出現紀錄資料是 GBIF 上資料筆數最多的資料集類型。\n"
"典藏資料（也就是標本）、文獻資料以及實地調查資料都可以透過此模板在 GBIF 上分享，此模板以觀察到的個體或採集到的標本為核心。\n"
"在這類資料集中，只要每個紀錄都有唯一識別碼，同一分類群可以有多個個體或標本。\n"
"出現紀錄資料的其他欄位則包括每個紀錄的觀察或採集地點、時間、方式以及採集者或觀察者。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:72
#, no-wrap
msgid "Event data is a class of datasets that was added on GBIF.org in 2015.\n"
"They allow data publishers to share more information about the context of the observation or collection of specimens, and to group occurrences linked to the same event.\n"
msgstr "調查活動資料是在 2015 年於GBIF.org新增的一類資料集。\n"
"它讓資料發佈者分享更多關於觀察或標本採集的背景資訊，並將相關聯的出現紀錄歸類到同一次調查活動中。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:77
#, no-wrap
msgid "The GBIF template for Event data allows data publishers to share more information about the context of a biodiversity data collecting/recording event such as camera traps, insect traps, botanical relevés, birding sites and so on.\n"
"Its structure is a bit more complex than the Taxonomical datasets and Occurrence datasets as it involves two files (e.g., two tabs in a spreadsheet): one for describing the ‘events’ (e.g. each trap) and the other one to describe the specimens or occurrences linked to each event.\n"
msgstr "GBIF 的調查活動資料模板讓資料發佈者分享更多關於生物多樣性資料採集或紀錄調查活動的背景資訊，例如相機陷阱、昆蟲陷阱、植物樣地調查、賞鳥地點等。\n"
"此模板的結構比物種名錄資料集與出現紀錄資料集稍微複雜，因為它包含兩個檔案（例如：試算表中的兩個分頁）── 一個用來描述「調查活動」（例如每個陷阱），另一個用來描述與每個調查活動相關聯的標本或出現紀錄。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:79 scripts/definitions.en.txt:144
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:131 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:190
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:151 scripts/structures.en.txt:95
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 15\n"
msgstr "// Slide 15\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:81
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sometimes, additional information or data can be found using other source materials than those previously mentioned in this video.\n"
msgstr "有時，除了本影片先前提到的資料來源外，還可以透過其他來源取得額外的資訊或資料。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:86
#, no-wrap
msgid "These other sources include remote sensing tools, maps and satellite data, and other information supports such as pictures, audio and video recordings of wildlife.\n"
"Most of the time, the information retrieved this way is supplemental to existing data but can provide valuable information about individuals and their location, habitat, migratory routes, etc.\n"
msgstr "這些其他來源包括遙測工具、地圖與衛星資料，以及其他資訊輔助資料，例如野生動物的照片、音檔與影片紀錄。\n"
"大多數情況下，透過這些方式取得的資訊是對現有資料的補充，但可以提供有關個體及其位置、棲息地、遷徙路線等有價值的資訊。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the following slides, we will see how the Darwin Core standard works with different types of data, and how to choose the dataset class that best fits a given dataset.\n"
msgstr "在接下來的投影片中，我們將說明達爾文核心標準如何應用於不同類型的資料，以及如何選擇最適合特定資料集的資料類別。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "As you learned in the Foundations - Darwin Core presentation, Darwin Core is a data sharing standard developed by the Biodiversity Information Standards (TDWG) organization, and is widely used to publish data within the extended GBIF community.\n"
"Depending on the type of dataset with which you work, you’ll have to choose one of its three Cores (Occurrence, Taxon or Event Core), which can be linked to one or more Extensions to share more information (such as multimedia links, variables, identification history and so on).\n"
"In some cases, a core can be used as an extension linked to another core: that is the case when publishing Event data, where one can use the Event Core to describe the sampled areas and then use the Occurrence Core as an Extension to share the specimens or individuals recorded.\n"
msgstr "如同在「基礎知識 - 達爾文核心標準」簡報中所學，達爾文核心標準是由生物多樣性資訊標準組織（TDWG）所制定的資料分享標準，並廣泛用於GBIF社群的資料發布。\n"
"根據所使用的資料集類型，需選擇三個核心資料集之一（出現紀錄、物種名錄或調查活動的核心資料集），並可連結一個或多個延伸資料集以分享更多資訊，例如多媒體連結、變數、鑑定史等。\n"
"在某些情況下，核心資料集也可以作為延伸資料集來連結至其他核心資料集。例如在發布調查活動資料時，可以使用調查活動核心資料集描述採樣區域，再利用出現紀錄核心資料集作為延伸資料集，用以分享觀察到的標本或個體紀錄。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:98 scripts/definitions.en.txt:189
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:169 scripts/resourcing.en.txt:199
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:116
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 19\n"
msgstr "// Slide 19\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "Once the data has been standardized using the Darwin Core standard, each class of dataset (metadata-only, sampling event, taxonomic or occurrence datasets) can be published on GBIF.org using one of the tools mentioned on this figure.\n"
"The most frequently used tool within the GBIF community is the GBIF-developed Integrated Publishing Toolkit (IPT), which transforms the source data into Darwin Core archives that are then harvested and indexed by GBIF.\n"
"The data are then searchable and downloadable via GBIF.org.\n"
msgstr "一但資料透過達爾文核心標準標準化後，每一類型的資料集（詮釋資料、調查活動、物種名錄或出現紀錄資料集）皆可透過此圖中所示的工具之一發佈至 GBIF.org。\n"
"在 GBIF 社群中，最常使用的工具是由 GBIF 開發的整合發佈工具包(IPT)，該工具能將原始資料轉換為達爾文核心集檔案，再由 GBIF 擷取與索引。\n"
"完成後，這些資料即可在 GBIF.org 上被搜尋與下載。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:104 scripts/definitions.en.txt:201
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:176 scripts/resourcing.en.txt:214
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:287
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 20\n"
msgstr "// Slide 20\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing a dataset class can take some time, especially if you’re new to data publishing within GBIF.\n"
"The GBIF helpdesk team created this useful flowchart which can be found on the GBIF Data Blog: \n"
msgstr "選擇合適的資料集類型可能需要一些時間，特別是當你剛開始在 GBIF 平台上發佈資料時。\n"
"GBIF 技術支援團隊製作了一份實用的流程圖，可在 GBIF Data Blog 上找到\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:109
#, no-wrap
msgid "If your data are not yet captured in a database or spreadsheet, or if they are too incomplete to be shared yet, you can publish a description of your data as a metadata-only dataset (please note this option is not allowed in the context of the BID and BIFA programmes).\n"
msgstr "如果資料目前還沒有整理成資料庫或試算表的形式，或是內容尚未完整到可以分享，可以先發布一份資料描述，也就是僅含詮釋資料的資料集。（請注意，在 BID 和 BIFA 計畫中，這個選項是不被允許的）。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:111
#, no-wrap
msgid "If your dataset contains a list of scientific names that are not referring to observed and/or collected organisms or specimens, you can publish it as a taxonomical resource, or checklist.\n"
msgstr "如果你的資料集裡的學名並不是指實際觀察到或採集到的生物或標本，那麼你可以把它發佈成分類學資源或物種名錄。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:113
#, no-wrap
msgid "If your dataset includes scientific names linked to observed and/or collected specimens, as well as dates and locations, you can share it as an occurrence dataset.\n"
msgstr "如果資料集中的學名對應到實際觀察或採集的標本，並附有觀察或採集的日期與地點，就可以將它發佈為出現紀錄資料集。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:115
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lastly, if information about the protocols used on the field are available, you can choose to share your dataset as a sampling-event dataset.\n"
msgstr "最後，如果資料中包含現場採樣所使用的調查方法或流程資訊，你就可以將它發佈為調查活動資料集。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:117 scripts/definitions.en.txt:210
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:182 scripts/structures.en.txt:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 21\n"
msgstr "// Slide 21\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:121
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each class of dataset shared within GBIF has to meet its own data quality requirements.\n"
"Some requirements are mandatory (such as the occurrenceID, taxonID or eventID, depending on the type of dataset), others are optional but strongly recommended (such as coordinates in decimal degrees).\n"
"You can find these requirements on GBIF.org from the How-to menu.\n"
msgstr "在 GBIF 中，每一類資料集都必須符合其特定的資料品質要求。\n"
"有些欄位是必填的，例如出現紀錄 ID、物種 ID 或調查活動 ID，視資料集類型而定；有些則為選填，但強烈建議提供，例如十進位制座標。\n"
"這些要求可以在 GBIF 網站的操作指南選單中查詢。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:123 scripts/organizing.en.txt:191
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:143
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 22\n"
msgstr "// Slide 22\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:125
#, no-wrap
msgid "In order to be able to publish data to GBIF, it is important to understand the differences between the dataset classes at the early stages of the data capture and data managing process.\n"
msgstr "為了能夠將資料發佈到 GBIF，在資料收集與管理的早期階段就了解各類資料集之間的差異非常重要。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "You can reflect on this topic with the following questions:\n"
msgstr "你可以透過以下問題來思考這個主題：\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:129
#, no-wrap
msgid "Is your data type different than you originally thought?\n"
msgstr "資料的類型是否與最初的判斷不同？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "With what kind of data do you work?\n"
msgstr "你在處理的是哪一類型的資料？\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "How would you publish them to GBIF? (using which Core and/or extension?)\n"
msgstr "你會如何將這些資料發佈到 GBIF？會使用哪一個核心資料集或延伸資料集\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:135 scripts/organizing.en.txt:203
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:150
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 23\n"
msgstr "// Slide 23\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/data-types.en.txt:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation was originally created and narrated by Sophie Pamerlon with additional contributions by BID and BIFA Trainers, Mentors and Students.\n"
msgstr "本簡報最初由 Sophie Pamerlon 製作並講述，並由 BID 與 BIFA 的講師、導師及學生提供額外貢獻。\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "This session is foundation work and part of the Terminology section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will be introducing you to the language, terminology and definitions for some of the basic concepts, functions and processes that you are going to be putting into use during the rest of the course.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "First let’s review the structure of this session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:12
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the section on DEFINITIONS we will first try to clarify the use of the word “Data”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will then look at the terminology surrounding Fields, Labels and Lists.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "And lastly, we will straighten out some concepts around characters and their mappings that will help prevent unwanted data messes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "First definitions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:22
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this section we will look at some of the many different terms and phrases that exist within the world of data and information management. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will be using these throughout the course and it is important that you be familiar with, when and how to use them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:28
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s focus for a moment on some common words and phrases that contain the word data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:32
#, no-wrap
msgid "All of the phrases here include the word data, but are they all the same, and if not, then what is the difference?  \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "So, what is a “Database”?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:38
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are many definitions, but what they all have in common is the theme that a database is a structured and organized collection of data and/or information held on a computer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:40
#, no-wrap
msgid "Everything else in the list is just a way to help you achieve this data nirvana! \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s dig a little deeper then into those other terms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:46
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let us take a look at the phrase “database language”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:48
#, no-wrap
msgid "According to the Oxford English dictionary “language” is the method of human communication, […], consisting of the use of words [or symbols] in a structured and conventional way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:50
#, no-wrap
msgid "For our purposes, database languages and their close allies the array languages are the way by which a human communicates with a computer to manipulate and move around data. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:52
#, no-wrap
msgid "Examples that you may have come across are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:54
#, no-wrap
msgid "Java, SPARQL, SQL and Python\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "They are simply methods used to define and create tables, views and queries and also allow users to access, update, store and move data around.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:60
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now let’s look at the phrase “database program or software”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "Again going to the Oxford English dictionary a “program/programme” is a set of related measures or activities with a particular long-term aim.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this context, a “database program or software” combines and presents functions and features for manipulating data, together is a unified interface. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "Examples that you may come across are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "Microsoft Access, FilemakerPro and Oracle\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:70
#, no-wrap
msgid "So, database programs or software are grouped utilities which allow a user, via a database language, to store, manage, query and report data held in the system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:74
#, no-wrap
msgid "The last term is “data cleaning tool”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data cleaning tools are systems whose utilities focus is on the manipulation of data with the aim of increasing fitness for use. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:78
#, no-wrap
msgid "Examples that you may be familiar or will learn about during the course are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "OpenRefine, MatLab  and spreadsheets\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "A data cleaning tool should assist the user in the discovery and repair of messy data in order to increase quality.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:86
#, no-wrap
msgid "Continuing on with some more terms that you should be familiar with we will look at the differences between the terms Field, Label and List.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "You will often hear reference made to field or column names and field labels or headings. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "Often they will be used interchangeably, however they are different things. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "Field or column names refer to the actual column in the database table. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "They are a often short, not always in plain language, sometimes computer generated; but in a properly formed database they are ALWAYS unique. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:98
#, no-wrap
msgid "Field names are almost always used by database languages, because the avoid ambiguity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:100
#, no-wrap
msgid "Labels and headers on the other hand, refer to the human readable versions of the field names. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "Most often you will see these in the graphical user interface of your software and on exports and reports. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:104
#, no-wrap
msgid "In some software you can in fact have different labels to the same field on a user by user or group basis!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:108
#, no-wrap
msgid "As you work with your data you will come across the phrases “drop-down list” and “controlled vocabulary” and on the surface they can appear to be the same. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:110
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indeed they are both powerful tools to help you keep your data clean, but they are different.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:112
#, no-wrap
msgid "A drop-down list, also sometimes called a lookup table is a data entry element that is created in your database software. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:114
#, no-wrap
msgid "It allows to you select a value from a predefined set of options. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:116
#, no-wrap
msgid "They can also be generated from the data itself on the fly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "A controlled vocabulary on the other hand, is a very strictly defined, domain specific set of values that should be applied to a particular field. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:120
#, no-wrap
msgid "They are an integral part of a well defined standard. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:122
#, no-wrap
msgid "Such values can be used in a drop-down list as mentioned previously.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:126
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this section we will look at what the term character means in relation to data and computers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:130
#, no-wrap
msgid "As a reminder computers “see” in binary. Everything that you type in, is held in memory as a series of ones and zeros. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "The computer does not “see” the repertoire of letters and symbols on your keyboard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:134
#, no-wrap
msgid "It needs a map to translate the character set that you would write or type, into its binary language and then back again to show it to you on the screen or a printer. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:136
#, no-wrap
msgid "This map is made up of two parts:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:138
#, no-wrap
msgid "The “coded character set” and \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:140
#, no-wrap
msgid "The “character encoding”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each symbol or glyph is assigned a numeric value and that number is then converted to binary.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:146
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this example we see how character encoding is used to map the capital letter A in the western alphabet.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:148
#, no-wrap
msgid "The ASCII value for the capital letter A is the decimal number 65 \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:150
#, no-wrap
msgid "which in HEXIDECIMAL is 041 \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:152
#, no-wrap
msgid "and in BINARY 01000001\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:154
#, no-wrap
msgid "Try mapping your own name to Binary using the link at the bottom of the screen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:158
#, no-wrap
msgid "In effect what is happening is that when you type into a keyboard and save a file the software that you are using will select an encoding to save with it. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "When you open that file again the software needs to know which encoding (map) to use to redisplay the information back to you correctly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:164
#, no-wrap
msgid "So why is this important to you as a data custodian?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:166
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s say that you translate the start of Hamlet’s soliloquy\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:168
#, no-wrap
msgid " ”To be or not to be, that is the question”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:170
#, no-wrap
msgid "into French and then save the file with a text editor that uses the encoding Latin-1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:172
#, no-wrap
msgid "Then you send it to a colleague to check the translation. Voila!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:176
#, no-wrap
msgid "When they open the file in MicroSoft Word this is what they see…\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:178
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mon dieu!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:180
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are some unexpected characters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:182
#, no-wrap
msgid "You forgot to tell your colleague that you where using Latin-1 as the encoding. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:185
#, no-wrap
msgid "MicroSoft Word on their end was set to use the encoding set Unicode UTF-8. \n"
"The maps did not match.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:187
#, no-wrap
msgid "Depending on the software the data may be irretrievably corrupted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:191
#, no-wrap
msgid "In review we have looked at the differences between the phrases:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:193
#, no-wrap
msgid "Database; database language; database program, data-cleaning tool;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "Field names & Field labels; Drop-down lists & Controlled vocabularies and between\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "Repertoires, Coded sets & Encodings \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:199
#, no-wrap
msgid "And shown that although the terms may seem similar the in fact have very different definitions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:203
#, no-wrap
msgid "Remember that:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:205
#, no-wrap
msgid "“..The first step in wisdom is to know the things themselves; this notion consists in having a true idea of the objects.” \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:208
#, no-wrap
msgid "We often take for granted that there is a whole language associated with biodiversity informatics that is not commonplace and has to be learned. \n"
"There is no shame in going back to basics and getting to grips with the vocabulary,  grammar and syntax just like with any new language.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:215 scripts/standards.en.txt:294
#, no-wrap
msgid "This video is part of a series of presentations used in the GBIF Biodiversity Data Mobilization course. \n"
"The biodiversity data mobilization curriculum was originally developed as part of the Biodiversity Information Development Programme funded by the European Union. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/definitions.en.txt:216 scripts/organizing.en.txt:298
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:220
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation was originally created and narrated by Sharon Grant with additional contributions by BID and BIFA Trainers, Mentors and Students.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this presentation, we will provide an overview of the importance of documentation as it relates to data management and data publishing.\n"
"You will learn about data mapping, data relationships and metadata .\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the previous Foundations presentations, we looked at the individual elements that make up both your data and the containers that hold it.\n"
"Now we’ll look at ways to document those data and what you want to do with it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:12
#, no-wrap
msgid "According to the director Steven Spielberg,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "“People have forgotten how to tell a story.\n"
"Stories don’t have a middle or an end any more.\n"
"They usually have a beginning that never stops beginning.”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "Documentation is the story of your project or dataset.\n"
"There should always be a concrete beginning, middle and end.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:21
#, no-wrap
msgid "In essence you should be able to answer certain questions at specific points:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "At the beginning: What did our project aim to do? \n"
"What was the purpose of creating the dataset?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:27
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the middle:  What did we actually do? \n"
"What did we achieve? \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "At the end: What didn’t we achieve? \n"
"Why? \n"
"What should happen next?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "In order to document your data and render them as fit for use as possible for yourself and future users, you need to follow these three essential steps :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mapping your data to existing formats and standards if necessary\n"
"Planning your data moves\n"
"Create useful metadata describing your data\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:44
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will begin by looking at ways to describe your data, then we will show how to map your data from one data structure to another and finally talk about how to record metadata about your data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:46
#, no-wrap
msgid "First is mapping data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:48
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is the process that you will use to describe how the attributes in your dataset can be transformed into attributes in a different one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:50
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mapping data is the “Process of Identifying the start field(s) within dataset A, and its (their) corresponding field(s) in dataset B.”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:52
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this example Dataset A has 10 columns.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:54
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are: \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "some integer fields represented by the purple squares, \n"
"some boolean fields represented by the red triangles\n"
"Some date fields represented by blue pentagons \n"
"Some text fields, green 8 pointed stars\n"
"and some complex fields\n"
" \n"
"// Slide 5\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dataset B has a different set of fields, 15 in total.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "What we need to do is identify which fields in Dataset A match which fields in Dataset B\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "We do that by describing the relationships.\n"
"For example Dataset A has an ID field and so does Dataset B.\n"
"We can draw a line directly between the two.\n"
"This is called a one to one match.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "Question: Can you see any other one to one matches? \n"
"Answer: State, Exp?, Pink Elephant!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "Another type of relationship is is a one to many.\n"
"In these cases there is a single field in dataset A that maps to 2 or more fields in Dataset B.\n"
"Date is an example.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:85
#, no-wrap
msgid "You many also find cases where a more than one field in Dataset A contains data for more than one field in Dataset B.\n"
"Notes fields are often guilty of this.\n"
" \n"
"// Slide 7\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "In fact there are six relationships:\n"
"Some fields will map one-to-one (1:1), meaning that the original column in dataset A exactly matches another one in dataset B\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some fields will map many-to-one (∞:1), meaning that some columns in dataset A can be merged (or concatenated) to match a single one in dataset B\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some fields will map one-to-many (1:∞), meaning that one column in dataset A will have to be split into two or more fields in order to match the fields in dataset B\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some fields might not exist yet zero-to-one (0:1), meaning that some information are not present in dataset A and need to be added in a new column to match an existing field in dataset B\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some fields might not have a place to go one-to-zero (1:0), meaning that some information present in dataset A does not match any existing field on dataset B\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:98
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some fields will map many-to-many (∞ : ∞), meaning that information in dataset A is scattered in different fields, which do not match exactly existing ones in dataset B\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:101 scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:113
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:109
#, no-wrap
msgid " \n"
"// Slide 8\n"
msgstr " \n"
"// Slide 8\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:103
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each of the relationship types has its own nuances as to how you are going to have to handle them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "one-to-one (1:1) - Beware your field types. \n"
"If they are the not the same. \n"
"You will need to do some manipulation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:109
#, no-wrap
msgid "many-to-one (∞:1) - These fields will need to joined together to go into the field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:111
#, no-wrap
msgid "one-to-many (1:∞) - These fields will need to be split apart and put in to different fields\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:115
#, no-wrap
msgid "zero-to-one (0:1) - Work out how (or if) you can even fill these in.\n"
"Is the data mixed into a notes field? \n"
"If so how do you get it out?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "one-to-zero (1:0) - Either throw away the data or add a field.\n"
"You might not even have a home in the other dataset.\n"
"Do/Can you create a new field?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:122
#, no-wrap
msgid "many-to-many (∞ : ∞) -  “for the love of a higher being!” \n"
"This usually means that your incoming data is very messy and will take time to clean before you can map it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:124
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 9 \n"
msgstr "// Slide 9 \n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is an example of what a mapping document looks like.\n"
"Notice that not only do we document the relationships we also articulate (to the best of our knowledge) what should be done to the data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "We’ve talked a lot about documenting the structures that hold your data but you must also document the information that gives context.\n"
"This is called metadata and is data about your data!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:135
#, no-wrap
msgid "As we mention in the section on data quality your ”Metadata must be rich enough to allow data (re)use by a third party without them having to refer to the data source.”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:139
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will discuss this in more detail in later sessions but as a quick guide good documentation should include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:141
#, no-wrap
msgid "A title – Which should be descriptive, memorable and if possible unique.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:143
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dates are good to include in a dataset name for example to allow you to track versions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:146
#, no-wrap
msgid "A narrative – Should describe the rationale for the creation of the dataset.\n"
"It should include at least general information about spatial, temporal and taxonomic coverage and give the potential user a broad picture of uses that may be appropriate for the data without further transformation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:148
#, no-wrap
msgid "Source information – If you did not collect or measure the data yourself, where or who did you get if from?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:151
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lineage – Does the dataset have a history? \n"
"Have any of the fields been transformed in any way from the original?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:153
#, no-wrap
msgid "Statement of accuracy – Using the concepts of accuracy, precision, errors and uncertainty that we discussed previously, are there any issues with the dataset that should be known to a user?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dates and life expectancy – When will the dataset be available? \n"
"How long is it valid for? \n"
"When if ever will it be updated?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:161
#, no-wrap
msgid "Field Definitions – Describing the format of fields and what kind of data each contains.\n"
"Was any cleaning or transformation down to the original data? \n"
"This is important for you to know if you are mapping it to a standard such as Darwin Core.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:164
#, no-wrap
msgid "Collection methodology – How was the data collected? \n"
"Where there protocols used which will affect its fitness for use?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:166
#, no-wrap
msgid "Statement of completeness – What is missing from the data and why?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:169
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditions of use and constraints – Where and how can you use the data? \n"
"For most portals and GBIF in particular data must use very open licensing, so understanding the requirements of data owners and their institutions is very important.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:172
#, no-wrap
msgid "Custodianship/Contact information  – This should be the institution responsible for the dataset as presented to you, as well as (if possible) the individual who created the dataset.\n"
"There should also be a technical contact who is responsible for the publication of the dataset.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/documentation.en.txt:180
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation was originally created by Sharon Grant and has been narrated by Sophie Pamerlon.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "This session is part of the planning section. \n"
"We will be introducing some concepts and methodologies that will help you to focus on the organization of your mobilization project at a number of levels. \n"
"The focus in this session will be on tasks and the ways in which they can be organized in order to prepare practical, workable plans and clear documentation. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "The successful implementation of your project is dependent in large part on the development of realistic, efficient and streamlined processes and documentation\n"
"This, combined with constant assessment of changes to the landscape within which your project exists, will increase drastically the likelihood of success.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this section we will look at three methodologies for clustering the timing and ordering of the tasks and components identified as critical to the goals of the project\n"
"We will then see how good planning is supported by continuous documentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s take a quick look at the definition of the noun cluster.\n"
"It is defined as “A group of similar things or people positioned or occurring closely together”. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:21
#, no-wrap
msgid "The three important parts of the definition for us:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:23
#, no-wrap
msgid "Groups\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:25
#, no-wrap
msgid "Similarity\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:27
#, no-wrap
msgid "Things\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "What we will see is that in order to successfully achieve your mobilization goals, you need to be able to identify groups and arrangements of similar tasks, cluster them together and then string those clusters together in a logical flow. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "In plain terms we will now be looking at the When and the How of your project\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s look first at GROUPINGS.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:37
#, no-wrap
msgid "Probably the most simplest grouping to be able to identify in your project are Stages relative to a fixed point in time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "What we mean here is, at what time during the project should any particular task (or group of tasks) occur? \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "The simplest but by no means the only way of stage-grouping, uses digitization as its reference point\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:46
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this example:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:48
#, no-wrap
msgid "Before - refers to any time before the start of the digitization effort itself ie the imaging and transcription;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:50
#, no-wrap
msgid "During - relates specifically to the period of active digitization; and \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:52
#, no-wrap
msgid "After - is the period after the digitization effort has ended. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "Another common reference point is the start and end of funding. \n"
"You can use anything that makes sense and even use different reference points for different stakeholders and groups of tasks, that run concurrently.\n"
"These points are often also good places to insert milestones with metrics into your project.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "The next set of groupings is PMBoK.\n"
"PMBoK stands for Project Management Body of Knowledge and it is the entire collection of processes, best practices, terminologies, and guidelines that are accepted as standards within the project management industry\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is overseen by the Project Management Institute (PMI), which is a global not-for-profit member association of Project Managers.\n"
"The first edition of the PMBOK Guide was published in 1996 and it is now on its fifth edition, which was published in 2013\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "As a very high level synopsis, the methodology uses iterative and repeatable groupings to cluster tasks together\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:69
#, no-wrap
msgid "The stages are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:71
#, no-wrap
msgid "INITIATING\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "PLANNING\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:75
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXECUTING\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:77
#, no-wrap
msgid "MONITORING and\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:79
#, no-wrap
msgid "CLOSING\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:84
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tasks carried out during the initiating phase should define the new project or the part of the project being tracked.\n"
"For example obtaining collection permits or MOUs before going into the field to collect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:89
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tasks carried out during the planning phase are required to establish the scope of the project.\n"
"For example assessing the physical state of a collection before pulling specimens for imaging.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tasks carried out during the executing phase are those that accomplish the project’s stated deliverables.\n"
"For example the transcription of label data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:99
#, no-wrap
msgid "The monitoring phase is an overarching and overlapping phase.\n"
"The tasks assigned to it serve the function of watchdog over the planning and executing phases, ensuring that performance is on track.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:103
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tasks in the closing phase indicate that goals and milestones have been achieved and the project (or part of the project in question) have finished.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "This diagram shows the continuous flow and cyclical nature of the five PMBoK phases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:113
#, no-wrap
msgid "Task clusters are a defined set of digitization task groups.\n"
"The term was coined by the iDigBio team in their 2012 paper “Five task clusters that enable efficient and effective digitization of biological collections”.\n"
"In the paper the authors identify 5 major groups of tasks that are most often found in successful natural history digitization projects. They are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:115
#, no-wrap
msgid "pre-digitization curation and staging\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:117
#, no-wrap
msgid "specimen image capture\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "specimen image processing\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:121
#, no-wrap
msgid "electronic data capture and\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:123
#, no-wrap
msgid "georeferencing locality descriptions\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:125
#, no-wrap
msgid "Clustering as general concept, is an extremely useful way to organize the detail of the tasks which must be carried out during any particular project stage and divides them up into manageable and trackable chunks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:129
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this diagram of the digitization task clusters you can see some of the checking and monitoring feedback loops seen in the PMBoK method.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "The next examples of clustering that we will look at are generally termed “Workflows”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:136
#, no-wrap
msgid "Workflows are defined as “the sequence of processes through which a piece of work passes from initiation to completion.”\n"
"There are three general categories: \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1) '
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:138
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sequential\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2) '
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:140
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Rules Driven \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3) '
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:142
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "State Machine\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:145
#, no-wrap
msgid "In simple terms they are just another way that you join tasks and task clusters together and apply rules and logic to increase efficiency.\n"
"We will look at the three workflows by describing in increasing detail, a common digitization workflow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:150
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sequential workflows are simple and represent a unidirectional series of steps with a single start and end point. \n"
"The important point being that each task MUST be completed before the next one can start.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:154
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this example the sequence is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME CHECKING\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:158
#, no-wrap
msgid "Followed by PHOTOGRAPHY\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "Then DATA ENTRY\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:162
#, no-wrap
msgid "And finally PROOFREADING\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:167
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rules driven workflows often have an underlying sequential element as described previously. \n"
"However, within each task cluster, rules and logic can alter the progress of the workflow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:171
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this example in the lower right corner in the PHOTOGRAPHY task cluster there is a quality check logic gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:174
#, no-wrap
msgid "If there is a problem with the image it is discarded and the specimen goes back to be photographed again.\n"
"Only when the quality check has been passed can the workflow continue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:180
#, no-wrap
msgid "Machine State workflows are the next most complex form.\n"
"Here, the steps within each task cluster can happen asynchronously i.e they are not necessarily performed one after another, but instead can be triggered by actions and states in previous steps.\n"
"One state is assigned as the start state, and then, based on an event, a transition is made to another state and so on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:185
#, no-wrap
msgid "If we take this example from Allard 2012.\n"
"Let’s say that the photography lab is only allowed to hold 60 herbarium sheets at any one time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:187
#, no-wrap
msgid "The NAME CHECKING task cluster can continue to prep specimens but there is a check that means: Specimens cannot pass to the PHOTOGRAPHY task cluster until there is space again.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:189
#, no-wrap
msgid "Meanwhile the other clusters can also carry on as long as they have specimens or images to work on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:193
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this next short section, ELEMENTS OF A GOOD PLAN we will look at how good documentation aids good planning. we will:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "highlight the benefits of good documentation;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "make connections between the proposal, planning and implementation documents;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:199
#, no-wrap
msgid "and show how the elements of each build together, thereby reducing the agony of the last \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:201
#, no-wrap
msgid "minute end of project report.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:205
#, no-wrap
msgid "The definition of a plan is a detailed proposal for doing or achieving something.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:207
#, no-wrap
msgid "Documentation of your project should describe not only what you planned to do but also what you actually did.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:209
#, no-wrap
msgid "Its primary purpose is to communicate with all stakeholders in order to provide an exact account of expectations, responsibilities and achievements.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:211 scripts/structures.en.txt:155
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 24\n"
msgstr "// Slide 24\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:213
#, no-wrap
msgid "Thorough documentation has many benefits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:215
#, no-wrap
msgid "It provides accountability - everyone should know what they are responsible for and also what others are responsible for.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:217
#, no-wrap
msgid "It ensures integrity - do what you say you will do, especially where sensitive data is concerned.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:219
#, no-wrap
msgid "It provides protection - a good plan helps you avoid scope creep and unexpected requests.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:221
#, no-wrap
msgid "It provides proof of compliance - allows you to show that you have followed the standards that you said you would\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:223
#, no-wrap
msgid "It enhances availability - your plan should be available, there shouldn't be anything hidden at this stage. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:225
#, no-wrap
msgid "It documents retention - How long will the data created by this project remain \"fresh\", what needs to happen to keep it up to date. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:227
#, no-wrap
msgid "It documents disposition - What is the ownership of the data created by the project? Who will be responsible for it after the end of the project.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:229
#, no-wrap
msgid "It encourages transparency - openly shared practical project plans feed back into the community and allow others to learn from your successes and failures.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:231
#, no-wrap
msgid "And it promotes teamwork - All stakeholders benefit from knowing how they fit into the bigger picture and the more of a sense of ownership that they, have the more likely your project is to succeed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:233 scripts/structures.en.txt:161
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 25\n"
msgstr "// Slide 25\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:235
#, no-wrap
msgid "All of the resource elements that we have discussed in the previous section come together in the project documentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:237
#, no-wrap
msgid "The elements of your documentation overlap, let’s have a look at where in the documentation cycle each element appears.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:239
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 26\n"
msgstr "// Slide 26\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:241
#, no-wrap
msgid "We won’t cover much about the detail of creating a proposal, except to say that if it got you funding then you did a good job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:243
#, no-wrap
msgid "The proposal will contain the high-level description of purpose and rationale and a good faith description of a workplan as achievable at the time of writing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:246
#, no-wrap
msgid "The planning document or implementation plan on the other hand should give everyone involved at the start of the project a complete guide to how the project will actually get done.\n"
"This is where you should include the practicalities, \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:248
#, no-wrap
msgid "workflows for specific goals\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:250
#, no-wrap
msgid "resource allocations to tasks\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:252
#, no-wrap
msgid "milestones and targets\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:254
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 27\n"
msgstr "// Slide 27\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:256
#, no-wrap
msgid "The planning document should be a living entity that the project manager revisits regularly as they keep track of milestones and approach checkpoints.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:258
#, no-wrap
msgid "Workflows should be adjusted and re-documented as the landscape changes, to make sure that interim goals are kept on track.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:261
#, no-wrap
msgid "If this is done, then Progress reports which occur at checkpoints throughout the project are easy to compile.\n"
"Metrics can be reported and any changes in the project landscape highlighted for risk mitigation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:263
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 28\n"
msgstr "// Slide 28\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:266
#, no-wrap
msgid "The compilation of the final report then becomes the less daunting task of summarizing the previous progress reports and the planning document.\n"
"In a circular fashion your planning document now can be used in your next project proposal and has the benefit of being a tried and tested successful plan.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:268
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 29\n"
msgstr "// Slide 29\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:270
#, no-wrap
msgid "To review, the sections, “Clustering the Components” and “Elements of a Good Plan” we looked at three ways to arrange tasks together into feasible workflows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:273
#, no-wrap
msgid "Groupings –are ways to divide a project into manageable stages based on specific criteria, for example time.\n"
"They are helpful ways to determine milestones in planning documents and mark progress for interim reports.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:275
#, no-wrap
msgid "Task Clusters – were used to describe one of the most common sets of natural history digitization tasks and their ordering.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:277
#, no-wrap
msgid "While Workflows – where explained as ways for you to connect tasks and task clusters into logical sequences with criteria and logic to describe the actualities of day-2-day project working.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:280
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lastly we looked at how project documentation is a continuous process that begins with the proposal and should be considered task in its own right.\n"
"Continuous refinement of your documentation is essential to the successful communication of your project at all stages and will help you and your team know what they should be doing at any particular time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:282
#, no-wrap
msgid "You will learn more about documentation of our actual data in further session in this course.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:284
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 30\n"
msgstr "// Slide 30\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:286
#, no-wrap
msgid "In chapter 4 of the Hobbit, Bilbo and the unfortunate Dwarves are captured by goblins, chained together in the dark and led deep into the caverns and tunnels of the Misty Mountains. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:290
#, no-wrap
msgid "This may indeed be your first reaction to starting out on your practical plan, but remember that being physically clustered together made it easier for the dwarves to stay together and they escaped.\n"
"It was by getting detached that Bilbo was lost.\n"
"Of course he found the ring as result, but that’s another story for another time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:292
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 31\n"
msgstr "// Slide 31\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/organizing.en.txt:297
#, no-wrap
msgid "If you have questions on this presentation, please use the provided forum in the e-Learning platform.\n"
" \n"
"This video is part of a series of presentations used in the GBIF Biodiversity Data Mobilization course.\n"
"The biodiversity data mobilization curriculum was originally developed as part of the Biodiversity Information Development Programme funded by the European Union.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this presentation, we will introduce you to the concept of Data Publishing.\n"
"We will also introduce you to GBIF’s Integrated Publishing Toolkit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "At this point in the course, you have moved through all the sections of mobilization project.\n"
"Now you are ready to share your data.\n"
"You’ve produced a cleaned and standardized dataset and you want to share and see that data on the GBIF data portal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "But there is more to it than simply handing over a spreadsheet or a text file to GBIF.\n"
"You must publish the data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data publishing is the act of making biodiversity datasets publicly accessible and discoverable, in a standardized form (ie Darwin Core), via an access point.\n"
"This access point is a URL – a web address.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "Most organizations within the GBIF Network, otherwise known as publishers, make use of an IPT, the Integrated Publishing Toolkit, to publish their data.\n"
"These publishers may choose to host their own installation of an IPT like the Field Museum, but generally prefer to find a suitable host for their data publishing activities.\n"
"This might be through an established GBIF Participant Node like GBIF France or through an established thematic group like VertNet.\n"
"Or, a another option, might be to make use of one of the GBIF-hosted IPTs like the BID, BIFA or regional IPTs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:26
#, no-wrap
msgid "If you are part of groups like Symbiota or the Living Atlases Communities, they have other means to assist you with publishing your data to GBIF.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "The definition on the previous Slide should probably be updated to include some words about waivers and licenses.\n"
"The act of not only making the data accessible, but making it as open as possible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "In an effort to document the level of openness of a dataset, GBIF maintains, in keeping with a 2014 decision by the GBIF governing board, that all data published and registered for use in the GBIF portal, must be issued with one of three Creative Commons options:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:37
#, no-wrap
msgid "CC0, a waiver for data made available for any use without any restrictions\n"
"CC BY, a license for data made available for any use with appropriate attribution\n"
"CC BY-NC, a license for data made available for any non-commercial use with appropriate attribution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that CC-BY-NC licences have a significant effect on the reusability of data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "GBIF encourages data publishers to choose the most open option they can wherever possible.\n"
"Projects supported through GBIF-led funding must use either the CC0 or CC BY options.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Integrated Publishing Toolkit (IPT) is a free, open source software tool written in Java that is used to publish and share biodiversity datasets through the GBIF network.\n"
"The first version of IPT was released in 2009.\n"
"It was redesigned and rereleased in 2011.\n"
"Since that time, the IPT has been updated periodically with new features, bug fixes and security improvements.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:51
#, no-wrap
msgid "While the IPT is maintained at GBIF, the community of biodiversity informatics developers are able to contribute to its development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:53
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some of the features include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:55
#, no-wrap
msgid "Main (but not only) publishing tool for GBIF.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:57
#, no-wrap
msgid "One IPT can host many datasets, on behalf of several institutions, each clearly represented\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:59
#, no-wrap
msgid "Test mode and production mode\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "Multilingual – it has been translated into 7 languages.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is a server-side software, so it needs a stable connection and requires technical administration.\n"
"This is why many institutions choose to publish their data on hosted IPTs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "You can find more information on data hosting on the GBIF website under the How-to menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "For the remainder of our course, we’ll be demonstrating and completing exercises using the IPT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:72
#, no-wrap
msgid "We’re now putting together what you learned in the first two presentations in the Data Capture section on Standards and Darwin Core and Data Types.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:75
#, no-wrap
msgid "At its heart, the IPT is based on Darwin Core and the Darwin Core text guide and its recommendations for sharing data in a Darwin Core Archive.\n"
"The Darwin Core contains three distinct data cores which match up to three of the dataset types that GBIF accepts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:77
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keep in mind:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:79
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each dataset has one and only one Core file\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:81
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each Core file corresponds to a dataset type: occurrence, checklist or sampling event.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:83
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each dataset can have one or more extensions file\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:85
#, no-wrap
msgid "The aim of an extension is to add new data fields not present in the core.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each entry in the core file is linked to zero, one or several rows in an extension file.\n"
"Each row in an extension file reference one and only one row in the core file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some examples:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "Core is Occurrence, no extensions\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "Core is Occurrence, with “multimedia” extension\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "Core is Occurrence, with Audubon core and measurementorfact extensions\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:98
#, no-wrap
msgid "Core is Taxon, no extensions\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:100
#, no-wrap
msgid "Core is Taxon, with species distribution extension\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "Core is Event with Occurrence and Measurementorfact extensions\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:110
#, no-wrap
msgid "The first core is the Occurrence Core.\n"
"As a reminder, an occurrence dataset contains one individual or one group of individuals.\n"
"Each row has a unique identifier, an occurrenceID.\n"
"Other fields for occurrence data include where, when, how and by whom was each occurrence observed and/or collected in the field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "The next option is the Taxon core.\n"
"As a reminder, a checklist dataset contains taxonomic concepts, not individuals.\n"
"It is a “A catalogue or list of named organisms, or taxa.” Each row will have a taxonID.\n"
"Each taxon must be unique.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:124
#, no-wrap
msgid "The last option is the Event core.\n"
"As a reminder, sampling event datasets allow data publishers to provide greater detail, not only offering evidence that a species occurred at a given location and date, but also making it possible to assess community composition for broader taxonomic groups or even the abundance of species at multiple times and places.\n"
"By indicating the methods, events and relative abundance of species recorded in a sample, these datasets improve comparisons with data collected using the same protocols at different times and places—in some cases, even leading researchers to infer the absence of particular species from particular sites.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each row will have an EventID.\n"
"Each event must be unique.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is a good point to review all of the fields required by GBIF.\n"
"If required fields are not supplied, GBIF will have difficult indexing your dataset.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:135
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each core has its own set of required and recommended fields for the dataset and for the associated dataset metadata.\n"
"As you now know, metadata is the data about your data and it enables users to know if your dataset will be fit for their use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:138
#, no-wrap
msgid "Don’t stop at the required and recommended fields.\n"
"The more data you are able to share, the more useful your dataset can be to end users.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:140
#, no-wrap
msgid "You can find the data quality on the gbif website under the How-to menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "Extensions were introduced in the Standards presentation.\n"
"Publishing a dataset is now where you will put them into use.\n"
"As mentioned previously, GBIF maintains the list of approved and draft extensions on its tools subsite.\n"
"The approved extensions can be enabled by an IPT administrator on a Production IPT.\n"
"Draft extensions can be enabled by an IPT administrator on a Test IPT.\n"
"If you know you have data that cannot be shared with Simple Darwin Core, it is worth reviewing the list of available extensions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:152
#, no-wrap
msgid "If you think an extension doesn’t exist, try reaching out to members of the TDWG community or posting on the Darwin Core issues site.\n"
"Community members will help guide you.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "Also, in the standards presentation, you were introduced to Darwin Core Archives.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:158
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is an example of a Darwin Core archive with extensions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "It’s an occurrence dataset that has been published with images, determination history and genebank information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:165
#, no-wrap
msgid "Until now, it may have been difficult to imagine how two single files can be related.\n"
"A core with an extension can represent the following relationships.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:167
#, no-wrap
msgid "1:0\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:169
#, no-wrap
msgid "1:1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:171
#, no-wrap
msgid "1:many\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:177
#, no-wrap
msgid "The core file will always contain unique records.\n"
"In this example using the Taxon Core, each taxon is unique and is represented by a taxonID.\n"
"In Vernacular Names extension file, each vernacular name is unique.\n"
"The first three represent common names for the species Struthio camelus (1:many relationship).\n"
"The fourth name represents the common name for the species Alectoris chukar (1:1 relationship).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:182
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this example the third and fourth taxons in the core file, do not have a match in the extension file.\n"
"(1:0 relationship).\n"
" \n"
"// Slide 14\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:185
#, no-wrap
msgid "As we finish up this presentation, we’d like for you to begin exploring the IPT.\n"
"In the e-Learning platform, you will find your login information and the location of the test IPT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:188
#, no-wrap
msgid "Login and a take a look around.\n"
"In the next presentation, you will watch a demonstration on how to use the IPT, after which you’ll be ready to try it yourself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:192 scripts/standards.en.txt:289
#, no-wrap
msgid "Thank you.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:199
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation was originally created by Sophie Pamerlon with additional contributions by Nicolas Noe, Laura Anne Russell and Dag Endresen, BID and BIFA Trainers, Mentors and students.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/publishing-concepts.en.txt:200 scripts/standards.en.txt:297
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation has been narrated by Laura Anne Russell.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "This session is part of the planning section.\n"
"We will be introducing some concepts and methodologies that will help you to focus on the organization of your mobilization project at a number of levels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "Good planning begins by defining the elements that will have an impact on your project and then understanding the interactions between them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this section we will look at the different players that it is necessary to understand before you can effectively organize your project and a methodology to identify WHO the key players and resources are and WHAT they should be doing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:13
#, no-wrap
msgid "The 5 key concepts we will look at are: Affiliations, Stakeholders, Roles, Tasks and Goals.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "First let us take a moment to understand the term Mobilisation.\n"
"Mobilisation is the action of bringing resources into use to achieve a particular purpose or goal.\n"
"In other words, to mobilize something, whether it be an expedition to the Antarctic or the publication of data, you will need to bring together available resources and assets and use them to achieve a defined “something”.\n"
"To mobilize we must understand our resources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:27
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s break this down this and look at the first part of the definition of the term mobilize and dig into what a RESOURCE is.\n"
"In the context of biodiversity data mobilization there are two types of resource.\n"
"The hardware that you will need to create or manipulate digital files (for example computers, cameras, lights, desks etc) and the people.\n"
"The actual individuals who will work with and for the project.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:34
#, no-wrap
msgid "Specific hardware choices are unfortunately, outside of the scope of this presentation, but there are references to existing resources in the e-Learning platform, that may be helpful.\n"
"Don’t be shy to ask your mentors and colleagues who have done similar projects for help.\n"
"In general though, which hardware you need, depends on both the type of digitization being carried out (for example herbarium sheets or pinned insects etc) as well as the combination of people who will do the digitization and publication.\n"
"Do NOT fall into the trap of buying equipment before identifying the people. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "So to the people.\n"
"People come in many types and can be organized in may ways, and it is the connections between that will have the most impact on the successful completion of your mobilization project.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "They can be broadly grouped into 3 levels: AFFLIATIONS, STAKEHOLDERS and ROLES.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:44
#, no-wrap
msgid "Affiliations are very high level and most broadly represent the entity that is responsible for the management and pay of the team or individual.\n"
"They are often strategic in nature.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "Stakeholders are groups or teams who may or may not be part of a formal management structure.\n"
"They share an affiliation and a tactical theme.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "Roles in this context, represent jobs or positions with a title that an individual may hold.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:53
#, no-wrap
msgid "Three affiliations are commonly a part of the biodiversity data mobilization landscape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "An “INSTITUTIONAL” affiliation usually accounts for teams who work for the institution that is running the project.\n"
"In the context of the project they are NOT specifically paid by the project. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:58
#, no-wrap
msgid "A “PROJECT” affiliation covers those teams that ARE specifically paid by the project for any part of their time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "Those teams or groups who do not work for the institution and are not paid by the project specifically, but whose input is required for success, have an “EXTERNAL” affiliation.\n"
"Three often encountered examples of these are Local Government, Regional Government and NGOs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "Knowing the affiliations of the teams working on your project will give you a good idea of any potentially competing or supporting strategic goals, priorities, projects and agenda’s.\n"
"It is often at this level that long to medium term plans and policies come into play, such as Laws, Intellectual Property Rights, and Retention Policies which can have a high impact on your project and its goals.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:72
#, no-wrap
msgid "Stakeholders represent the next level of the WHO in your project. \n"
"These are most often the tactical units within a shared affiliation.\n"
"They have responsibility for day to day running such as managing infrastructure, curating the collection, doing the research and paying the bills.\n"
"Stakeholders may or may not be part of the same formal management structure but they share an affiliation and come together to do something practical.\n"
"It is important to remember that no two affiliations organize their stakeholder groups in the same way you should never assume that someone else’s structure is the same as yours.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:81
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each stakeholder group is made up of one or more Roles.\n"
"Roles in this context represent jobs or positions with a title that an individual may hold.\n"
"Each of these roles is held by a real person who will have at least one role.\n"
"It is important to understand how these roles are distributed across the different stakeholder groups.\n"
"Be aware that a named role at your institution may not be in the same stakeholder group somewhere else.\n"
"In some case even though the name of the role may be the same they may have different responsibilities.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:86
#, no-wrap
msgid "There is one particular role that we should spend a little time on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "The project manager.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is the person who must: \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keep track of Scope, Manage, control and mitigate Change of everything.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "Articulate and keep track of the planning process by turning high-level information into detailed executable plans.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "Follow through on the Management of the project team so they can meet the project objectives.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:98
#, no-wrap
msgid "Monitor and control the work of producing the products, and results that the project has committed to and ultimately be responsible for the Success of the project as measured by quality, timeliness and budget compliance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "The second element of the definition of the term mobilize is USES.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:104
#, no-wrap
msgid "These work tasks represent WHICH things you will need to do, in order to successfully do what you said you would do. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "The specific set of tasks carried out by the individuals assigned to the roles and working on your project, is dependent on the type of digitization that you need to do in order to create your publishable dataset. \n"
"However, they will most likely include some or all of the following tasks: Specimen/Object Staging, Curation, Data Review, Database Review, Image Capture, Transcription, Data Cleaning, Quality Checking, Georeferencing and Publishing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:114
#, no-wrap
msgid "Once identified, tasks will need to be scheduled and ordered to be effective.\n"
"There are many way to arrange tasks, some of course will be more efficient than others.\n"
"We will look more closely at the application of several methods for manging TASKS for data mobilization in the session on ORGANISING.\n"
"These will include: Groupings, Clustering and Workflows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:117
#, no-wrap
msgid "Remember that whilst there is no perfect ”one size fits all”, digitization activities most have been tried at least once by someone else! \n"
"There are many existing repositories of digitization protocols available online, and many are included in the course resources in the eLearning platform.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:121
#, no-wrap
msgid "The third and final element of the definition is the outcome or outcomes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "GOALS are the “WHAT are you going to do” of the project, and will have been established in the original project proposal.\n"
"In fact this is actually the place to start.\n"
"Clearly re-articulating the stated deliverables of a project and when they are due is the critical first step in creating a successful implementation plan.\n"
"Time should also be spent documenting any implied goals.\n"
"Whilst these are often more difficult to metricize, they often speak directly to the sustainability of the project and its impact outside of its target audience.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "Examples of explicit stated data mobilization goals are: \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "Publishing Data to GBIF and doing it regularly; \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:135
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inclusion of additional supporting metadata over and above the basic fields if available; \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:137
#, no-wrap
msgid "Taking Images of an object/specimen from from multiple angles, or using advanced techniques such as; CT Scanning or Electron Microscopy; \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:139
#, no-wrap
msgid "Imaging all specimens in a collection as opposed to just of type specimens;  \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:141
#, no-wrap
msgid "Georeferencing rather than verbatim transcription of localities; Creating and Maintaining your own IPT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:143
#, no-wrap
msgid "More implied but equally important goals include: \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:145
#, no-wrap
msgid "Increased public awareness of local issues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:147
#, no-wrap
msgid "The ability to use a successful project as evidence of capacity and expertise when applying for further funding.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "Use of mobilised datasets to effect data driven policy change.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:153
#, no-wrap
msgid "You should break down high level implied or indirect goals into the direct deliverables that will move you towards your goals.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:155
#, no-wrap
msgid "For example if your goal is CAPACITY ENHANCEMENT then you could have specific achievements that may include setting up a regional IPT that can used by multiple institutions that cannot host their own or the training of collections staff to create and update their own ipt resources for publication.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "If the goal is INCREASED PUBLIC OUTREACH deliverables could include increased visitation to your collections web pages or increased number of observations by locals and citizen scientists. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:159
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this way it becomes possible to track the progress of the goal and ultimately report on its success.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:163
#, no-wrap
msgid "Having defined your goals, gathered your resources and organised your tasks you are now in a position to document the landscape and realistically assess where gaps, bottlenecks and barriers may exist in your implementation plan.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:166
#, no-wrap
msgid "One way to do this is take your list of existing stakeholders, associate roles to them and then assign to each role the tasks that you have decided they will need to carry out.\n"
"If any stakeholder or role seems to have too many tasks then this is likely to become a bottleneck in your project.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:169
#, no-wrap
msgid "A task with no assigned role indicates a a gap. \n"
"Effective early assessment of resources should mitigate capacity and practical barriers whilst identifying competing goals and conflicting affiliation will help to avoid cultural and institutional barriers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:171
#, no-wrap
msgid "You will see more on this process in the online planning exercises.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:175
#, no-wrap
msgid "In review, good planning begins by defining the elements that will have an impact on your project and understanding the interactions between them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:177
#, no-wrap
msgid "We have looked at the classifications of individuals and groups of people; and the ways in which they interact which each other that may support or block progress in the completion of your project.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:179
#, no-wrap
msgid "We then briefly discussed the need to identify the practical tasks that will need to be accomplished.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:181
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lastly, and arguably most significantly, we emphasized that a workable implementation plan begins with a thorough review of the goals and deadlines of the project.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:185
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using the criteria we have discussed you should now be able to assess the environment that your project exists within now in three steps: \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:188
#, no-wrap
msgid "First review your goals.\n"
"Do you know exactly what you need to do and when? \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:192
#, no-wrap
msgid "Second, research tasks similar to yours.\n"
"Has anyone else in the community done a similar project? \n"
"What did they use? \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lastly review available resources.\n"
"Start with the people.\n"
"Who do have to help you? \n"
"Only then see what equipment is already available and what you need to buy?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:203
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hopefully, the proposal project design, and the landscape in which is was scoped are still valid at implementation time.\n"
"This should however, never be taken for granted.\n"
"Things change.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:207
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is often the case that many months (and sometimes years) will have passed between the original submission, funding and project start.\n"
"The original project staff may even no longer be involved.\n"
"It is imperative therefore, that the deliverables as stated in the proposal are reviewed critically before implementation, before any hardware is bought or tasks begun.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:209
#, no-wrap
msgid "To paraphrase Gandalf in the book The Hobbit, you don’t need “a mighty warrior” or “even a Hero” to kill a dragon; you just need to “get on and make some plans”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:212
#, no-wrap
msgid "Whilst you may find some warriors to fight your cause, and a hero or two to help you out of a problem along the way, you shouldn’t rely only on them.\n"
"Knowing who the stalwart troopers and quietly reliable ones in your team is more likely to get you to the finish line on time, on target and within budget.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/resourcing.en.txt:219
#, no-wrap
msgid "If you have questions on this presentation, please use the provided forum in the e-Learning platform.\n"
" \n"
"This video is part of a series of presentations used in the GBIF Biodiversity Data Mobilization course. \n"
"The biodiversity data mobilization curriculum was originally developed as part of the Biodiversity Information Development Programme funded by the European Union.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "This session is foundation work and part of the Terminology section. \n"
"We will be introducing you to types of software used in data mobilization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "The purpose of this short presentation is to give you some examples of the different types of applications and software available in the world of biodiversity mobilization informatics. \n"
"Hopefully it will help you to start to think about what uses they may be put too and what types of things might be practical for you.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "So very briefly, a definition. \n"
"We are making a distinction here between all software and those applications which have been created for an end-user to carry out a task. \n"
"We will take a look at software that can be used for: Data Capture, Data Management, Data Cleaning and Data Publishing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the realm of data capture software, there is often no need to go \"big\" when capturing information initially, especially in the field. \n"
"You can capture information from collection specimens or live observations in a simple spreadsheet such as Excel, OpenOffice, LibreOffice or Google Sheets. \n"
"There may even not be a need to create your own system at all. \n"
"Apps such as iNaturalist, or eBird are stable, robust and have simple intuitive interfaces. \n"
"Whichever the choice, it is important to think about the long term preservation of the data that you collect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:32
#, no-wrap
msgid "So, depending on your needs, a more complex data management software might be the right solution for your data. \n"
"Especially if long term retention and preservation is a priority. \n"
"More stable management platforms also better support sharing of data to multiple audiences. \n"
"There are many choices which vary in terms of cost and support and a few are shown here.\n"
"From left to right: Symbiota, Specify, EMu, Elysia and Brahms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:37
#, no-wrap
msgid "If a home-grown solution is preferred and can be supported, relational database software can provide cheaper alternatives. \n"
"Examples incluse MySQL, SQLServer, Access or filemaker.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:48
#, no-wrap
msgid "Increasingly the lines between languages, programs and tools have blurred, each being capable of many complex and overlapping functions.\n"
"The common Find and Replace function is a good example of a data cleaning tool that is found in almost all database programs and is a command in database languages. \n"
"You are probably already familiar with spreadsheets such as Excel and indeed these are probably the most common data cleaning tools available. \n"
"However there are others. \n"
"OpenRefine originally known as GoogleRefine is a powerful free, open source data cleaning tool with a large support community. \n"
"You will see more of this program in upcoming sessions.  \n"
"R is another increasingly used, sophisticated programming language and environment. \n"
"With a little practice it can also provide powerful data manipulations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:57
#, no-wrap
msgid "Planning further ahead to the sharing of your data through GBIF to the international scientific and deciders community you will need to choose a publishing tool. \n"
"The choice will depend largely on the level of infrastructure support at your institution.\n"
"If this is high and stable, installing an IPT or BioCASe application may be the right option. \n"
"But there are others if this is not the case. \n"
"You can consider partnering with an existing ipt provider or selecting a database management system that includes a publishing pathway of its own. \n"
"Whichever you choose, make sure that your system allows you to export data as csv or txt files and you will ultimately be able to publish your data. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is a summary of the information collected in the course survey of software in use by students, mentors and trainers in the GBIF community. \n"
"The link and reference to more details of each section, is at the bottom of the slide.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "Remember that: The purpose of software is to help people and not the other way around. Your choice of solution should make your life easier but it should also not sacrifice the long term viability of the data that it is meant to work with.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/software.en.txt:72
#, no-wrap
msgid "If you have questions on this presentation, please use the provided forum in the e-Learning platform. \n"
"This video is part of a series of presentations used in the GBIF Biodiversity Data Mobilization course. \n"
"The biodiversity data mobilization curriculum was originally developed as part of the Biodiversity Information Development Programme funded by the European Union. \n"
"This presentation was originally created by Sophie Pamerlon and is narrated by Sharon Grant with additional contributions by BID and BIFA Trainers, Mentors and Students.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this presentation, we will introduce you to data standards as they relate to biodiversity data. \n"
"In particular, we will focus on the Darwin Core standard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "The engineer and industrialist W. Edwards Deming said:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:12
#, no-wrap
msgid "“Standardisation does not mean that we all wear the same colour and weave of cloth, eat standard sandwiches, or live in standard rooms with standard furnishing. \n"
"Homes of infinite variety of design are built with a few types of bricks, and with lumber of standard sizes, and with water and heating pipes and fitting of standard dimensions.” \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "What he was trying to say is that using standardization does not prevent us from being creative. \n"
"He was also showing us that we already live with standards all around us.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "As we move forward, we will define the term “Standard” and we’ll look at how we interact with standards every day. \n"
"We will then introduce you to biodiversity information standards, including the Darwin Core Standard with which you will continue to use throughout this course.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:22
#, no-wrap
msgid "So what is a Standard?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "At its simplest, it is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:26
#, no-wrap
msgid "“An agreed way of doing something.”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:30
#, no-wrap
msgid "Standards are combinations of norms, conventions, specifications, requirements, restrictions, and rules.\n"
" \n"
"// Slide 4\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "The main purpose for standards is to create a framework of “mutual understanding”. \n"
"They should provide clarity and help communication.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "Examples of everyday things that we encounter which make use of standards to aid communication of information are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:37
#, no-wrap
msgid "Units of Measurement\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "Numeral Systems\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "Alphabets\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:43
#, no-wrap
msgid "Languages\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:45
#, no-wrap
msgid "Emojis\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "Postal Addressing\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "Morse Code\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:51
#, no-wrap
msgid "Barcoding\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s take a very specific example and break it down. \n"
"Here in order to communicate accurately and repeatedly a position on the earth – a latitude and longitude – is actually a combination of at least 8 standards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:58
#, no-wrap
msgid "measurement - geographic coordinates \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:60
#, no-wrap
msgid "format - degrees, minutes, seconds\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "numeric system - sexagesimal\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "numbers - Indo-Arabic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "language - English\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "alphabet - Latin\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:70
#, no-wrap
msgid "symbols - typography\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:72
#, no-wrap
msgid "font - Roboto\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:78
#, no-wrap
msgid "Standards provide ways of constraining the array of possibilities. \n"
"In the earlier Foundations presentations, you learned about types of data, schemas, formats and character encodings. \n"
"Each of these can be used to constrain the array of possibilities within the terms of a Standard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "Types of data can restrict the values of a field. \n"
"So alphanumeric text in a text field, decimals in a float. \n"
"Yes/No in a Boolean.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:85
#, no-wrap
msgid "An encoding schema can restrict the range of values in a field. \n"
"For example, the list of possible latitude values have a range: between -90 and 90.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "A format can restrict the representation of a data in a field. \n"
"For example, whether a data appears as year month day, or day month year, or month day year.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:91
#, no-wrap
msgid "And then, character encoding provides the rules for interpreting bytes of data. \n"
"For our purposes, we’ll use UTF-8.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "During this course, you will be learning how to share your data. \n"
"As such, you will encounter standards for transferring data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:100
#, no-wrap
msgid "An application schema, allows for the combination of data standards for a specific purpose. \n"
"For example, using Darwin Core terms within Darwin Core Archives. \n"
"We’ll delve more into both in just a few moments.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:103
#, no-wrap
msgid "Again, you will make use of format, but this time the format restricts dataset structures. \n"
"A dataset may make use of csv, xml, json and rdf.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:106
#, no-wrap
msgid "And lastly, you will have a transfer protocol, which provides information on how and where to send information. \n"
"These might include http (hypertext transfer protocol), ftp (file transfer protocol), and smtp (simple mail transfer protocol or if you like, send mail to people).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:112
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the domain of biodiversity informatics there are many standards already available that can help you work with your data. \n"
"The USGS has a very specific definition of these:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:115
#, no-wrap
msgid "“Data standards are the rules by which data are described and recorded. \n"
"In order to share, exchange, and understand data, we must standardize the format as well as the meaning.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:117
#, no-wrap
msgid "The result of using these standards, where appropriate, is that you will increase data integrity, accuracy and consistency by clarifying ambiguous meaning and minimizing redundant data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "Those that you are likely to encounter or use on a regular basis may include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:121
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ecological Metadata Language Standard (EML) \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:123 scripts/standards.en.txt:236
#, no-wrap
msgid "Audubon Media Description (aka Audubon Core)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:125
#, no-wrap
msgid "Global Genome Biodiversity Network(GGBN)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ocean Data Standards and Best Practices Project (ODSBP)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:130
#, no-wrap
msgid "And last, but not least, we’ll spend the rest of this discussion on Darwin Core. \n"
"The Darwin Core standard will allow you to share your occurrence, taxonomic, and event datasets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:137
#, no-wrap
msgid "Darwin Core is a biodiversity standard developed by the Biodiversity Informatics community. \n"
"It was originally developed under the Taxonimic Databases Working Group or T D W G, pronounced TDWG. \n"
"In recent years, the group has been renamed, Biodiversity Information Standards. \n"
"But the acronym persists as the community is quite fond of the name TDWG.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:140
#, no-wrap
msgid "“The standard includes a glossary of terms (in other contexts these might be called properties, elements, fields, columns, attributes, or concepts) intended to facilitate the sharing of information about biological diversity by providing identifiers, labels, and definitions. \n"
"Darwin Core is primarily based on taxa, their occurrence in nature as documented by observations, specimens, samples, and related information.”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "So in short, Darwin Core is a “List of fields and their definitions, as they relate to biodiversity data.”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:145
#, no-wrap
msgid " \n"
"// Slide 10\n"
msgstr " \n"
"// Slide 10\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:148
#, no-wrap
msgid "As we dive deeper into Darwin Core or as it is abbreviated, DwC, you’ll learn it is more than JUST a list of fields. \n"
"We will use Simple Darwin Core which is a predefined subset of the terms that have common use across a wide variety of biodiversity applications.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:150
#, no-wrap
msgid "This subset contains greater than 150 fields, that are placed into a set of field classes comprised of:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:152
#, no-wrap
msgid "Record & Dataset\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:154
#, no-wrap
msgid "Occurrence\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "Organism\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:158
#, no-wrap
msgid "Material Sample\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "Event\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:162
#, no-wrap
msgid "Location\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:164
#, no-wrap
msgid "Geological Context\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:166
#, no-wrap
msgid "Identification\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:168
#, no-wrap
msgid "Taxon\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:170
#, no-wrap
msgid "Additionally, there are two auxiliary classes called:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:172
#, no-wrap
msgid "ResourceRelationship\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:174
#, no-wrap
msgid "MeasurementOrFact\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:176
#, no-wrap
msgid "From the Simple Darwin Core User Guide, “Simple Darwin Core is simple in that it assumes (and allows) no structure beyond the concept of rows and columns, which might be thought of as attributes and their values, or fields and records.”\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:182
#, no-wrap
msgid "The DwC Quick Reference Guide will soon become your go to resource. \n"
"This page provides a list of all currently recommended terms of the Darwin Core standard. \n"
"Categories such as Occurrence or Event correspond to Darwin Core classes which group other terms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:185
#, no-wrap
msgid " \n"
"// Slide 12\n"
msgstr " \n"
"// Slide 12\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:189
#, no-wrap
msgid "We now will look at some examples of Darwin Core terms. \n"
"The quick reference guide, consistently displays each term with the identifier name, definition, comments and examples. \n"
"The first terms we’ll review are Country and CountryCode within the Location category.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "Generally, data holders have a field for country within their source data. \n"
"But often this data can be quite messy with misspellings, abbreviations, and historical names. \n"
"However, it is one of the most easily standardized pieces of data. \n"
"As noted in the comments, the recommended best practice is to use a controlled vocabulary such as the Getty Thesaurus of Geographic Names. \n"
"A controlled vocabulary places restriction on the values that should be used for that term.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:198
#, no-wrap
msgid "CountryCode is a term that is generally not present in holder data. \n"
"But again, it is another field that can easily be supplied with data due to the recommendation in the comments to use an ISO 3166-1-alpha-2 country code.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:201
#, no-wrap
msgid "GBIF strongly recommends the sharing of CountryCode within occurrence datasets. \n"
"Sharing of Country is also encouraged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:203
#, no-wrap
msgid "You will learn more about the GBIF requirements and recommendations in later sessions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:211
#, no-wrap
msgid "The next term is basisOfRecord. \n"
"basisOfRecord defines the nature of each record in a dataset. \n"
"BasisOfRecord follows a controlled vocabulary. \n"
"You can choose from PreservedSpecimen, FossilSpecimen, LivingSpecimen, MaterialSample, Event, HumanObservation, MachineObservation, Taxon, or Occurrence. \n"
"GBIF requires basisOfRecord with published occurrence datasets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:225
#, no-wrap
msgid "The last term we will review is occurrenceID. \n"
"When publishing occurrence records, GBIF requires an occurrenceID. \n"
"An occurrenceID is an identifier for the occurrence itself, not the digital record of the occurrence. \n"
"Recommended best practice is to use a globally unique identifier otherwise known as a GUID. \n"
"In the absence of a GUID, a unique identifier can be comprised of other identifiers within the dataset. \n"
"For example institutionCode, collectionCode, and catalog number. \n"
"We call this a triplet. \n"
"There are tools on the internet that can help you to generate GUIDs for your records. \n"
"If you use this method, these GUIDs should become a permanent field within your source data identifying each record. \n"
"For the work done in this course, you will create a triplet. \n"
"The format will follow the third example here highlighted in red.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:228
#, no-wrap
msgid " \n"
"// Slide 15\n"
msgstr " \n"
"// Slide 15\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:234
#, no-wrap
msgid "In using Simple Darwin Core, you may discover that you have more data to share but you cannot find corresponding terms in DwC. \n"
"This data could be image or sound files or perhaps you are responsible for a collection of vertebrates and you have extensive data compiled on the weights and size of the specimens. \n"
"Or even detailed historical information on the identification of a taxon. \n"
"When this occurs, you will look to Darwin Core extensions so that you can extend the base data by providing additional files that correspond to the base data. \n"
"Extensions that would meet your needs in these three examples are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:238
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measurements or Facts\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:240
#, no-wrap
msgid "Identification History\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:243
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are many more extensions. \n"
"GBIF maintains a list of all approved and draft extensions on its tools subsite.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:248
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are many layers in our biodiversity informatics community. \n"
"The image here shows the relationships between these layers and where they intersect with the Darwin Core and where extensions maybe necessary in order to fully share data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:252
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data shared to GBIF is submitted via a Darwin Core Archive or DwCA\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:256
#, no-wrap
msgid "A DwCA is an expression of the Darwin Core text guide. \n"
"It is a compressed file containing a minimum of three files. \n"
"It is encoded as UTF-8. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:259
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this example, these three files are: \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1.	'
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:261
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A data file (occurrence.txt) conforming to the SIMPLEDWC in a CSV format where the first row includes Darwin Core standard term names.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2.	'
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:263
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A meta file (meta.xml) in XML format containing technical details to instruct a computer in how to use the data file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3.	'
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:265
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A meta file (eml.xml) in an XML format containing explanatory details about the records contained within the data file to instruct a user if the data will be fit for their use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:269
#, no-wrap
msgid "A more complex structure can be obtained by sharing multiple related csv files to extend the data. \n"
"They are related to the core file by way of a unique id. \n"
"In an occurrence dataset, these related csv files are related by the occurrenceID.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:272
#, no-wrap
msgid " \n"
"// Slide 18\n"
msgstr " \n"
"// Slide 18\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:274
#, no-wrap
msgid "We’ve covered What Darwin Core Is and hopefully you’ve started to develop a sense of why you should use it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:276
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is a standard and standards are good! Standards provide us with the rules and protocols we need to share our data with others.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:281
#, no-wrap
msgid "Darwin Core also provides us with a common language. \n"
"As we saw in the Foundations – Documentation presentation, source data can be tricky when trying to compare datasets. \n"
"The fields in your source data might be different than the fields in another institutions data source. \n"
"When we all use Darwin Core to share our data, we understand that data has been shared with a common language.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:285
#, no-wrap
msgid "// Slide 19\n"
"And it’s not only the data holders that understand this common language, the data users do as well. \n"
"And after all, what could be better than a user finding a data set that is fit for their use, shared in a common language, that allows them to do better science.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/standards.en.txt:296
#, no-wrap
msgid "This presentation was originally created by Paula Zermoglio and John Wieczorek with additional contributions by Sharon Grant, Sophie Pamerlon, Laura Anne Russell and Dag Endresen, BID and BIFA Trainers, Mentors and students. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "This session is foundation work and part of the Terminology section. \n"
"We will be introducing you to the language, terminology and definitions for some of the basic concepts, functions and processes that you are going to be putting into use during the rest of the course.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "First let us review the structure of this session. \n"
"In the section on STRUCTURES we will be working on understanding the field and data types that hold data, the structures that help to organize and protect that data and lastly look at what these mean for the integrity and security of your data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this section on basic database Structures, we will focus on the different elements that databases, software tools and languages use to handle the data that you enter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "First, let us look at field types, also known as data types.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:26
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data Types let the computer know what kind of data is expected in a particular field. \n"
"This then dictates what functions can be performed on it. \n"
"They can be simple and encompass a single concept or combine concepts depending on the software or language you are using. \n"
"Either way the essential building blocks are the same. \n"
"So let us look at six that you will come across most frequently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "First Numeric fields. \n"
"This data type holds numbers, and there are 2 kinds: Integers and long integers, and whole numbers. \n"
"For example 1, 2, 3, 99, 2000.\n"
"The size of the number, depends on the number of bits assigned to the field (more about that later). \n"
"For now just remember that a long integer field will hold a bigger number than an integer.\n"
"Floats, also referred to as doubles, hold fractions or parts of whole numbers. \n"
"For example 4.5, 19.47. \n"
"The important thing to remember here is that a float always has decimal places so the number 10 will be stored as 10.0. \n"
"The number of decimal places being an integral element of the type. \n"
"This has implications when it comes to precision and accuracy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:45
#, no-wrap
msgid "Alphanumeric fields are also referred to as text, character or string fields. \n"
"They can hold any letter, number or symbol in the coded character set and usually have a defined character limit. \n"
"Entering more characters than the character limit can often result in truncation and loss of data on save.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:52
#, no-wrap
msgid "Another type of text field that you will come across is the Unstructured text field, also known as memo, long char or blob fields. \n"
"These types of field are used to hold large amounts of unformatted text and unlike alphanumeric fields may not have an obvious character limit. \n"
"In simple systems, they are generally less flexible in terms of processing; for example sorting and indexing, than their smaller alphanumeric cousins. \n"
"With decreased storage costs and the rise of NoSQL document-oriented database programs, such as MongoDB however, this is changing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "System Value fields have a double nature and what you see on the screen is not usually the actual value that the software is storing in memory. \n"
"Date and time fields are the most common example of this type of field. \n"
"In this example you typed 1 March 1971 into a cell in Excel and hit return. \n"
"Now it says 1-Mar-71 in the cell AND 3/1/1971 in the functionbar.\n"
"Excel is applying a display format to the cell, which you can change at will. \n"
"HOWEVER, if you remove the format you see that what is actually being stored in the field is the number 25993.00! \n"
"So you can see that the field is actually a float. \n"
"You should beware because that system value not be the same from software to software.\n"
"Exports should to checked to make sure the actual date is exported and not the system value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:71
#, no-wrap
msgid "Boolean fields, also known as Binary fields, only contain one of two values that represent a one or a zero. \n"
"They are a special case of a System Value field as mentioned previously in that whilst the software actually saves a number it often displays values such as: Yes/No, Yes/no, Y/N, True/False.\n"
"You should take care to find out which way around the system you are using translates. \n"
"Is a 1 a yes or no?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:78
#, no-wrap
msgid "Structured Text fields are less common but mentioned here for completeness. \n"
"They are fields which can be either made up of combinations of the simple data types or even be defined by the data itself. \n"
"Most often these are used in programming languages to allow flexibility and handling of complex concepts. \n"
"An example is the factor field used in the R programming language, which is defined as a type vector containing a set of numeric codes with character-valued levels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now that you have an understanding of the field types used to represent your data elements let us talk about the containers or structures, that many database programs and software use collate and display your data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:87
#, no-wrap
msgid "First is the cell. \n"
"This is the smallest data structure, it holds a single value and has a single data type associated with it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:93
#, no-wrap
msgid "There is one concept that you should beware of when it comes to cells and that is the empty cell. An empty cell doesn't necessarily mean an empty field.\n"
"Some software fills an empty field with a System value based on the data type. \n"
"Examples are NULL N A or even zero!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:98
#, no-wrap
msgid "As you start to organize cells you begin to build a grid. \n"
"Grids have implied structure in the form of Rows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "And columns.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:109
#, no-wrap
msgid "When you organize cells into rows and columns, obviously, what you get is a grid!\n"
"This is looks very much like a spreadsheet right? \n"
"Well, yes. \n"
"The difference however between a spreadsheet grid and a table are the strength of the ties that hold the rows and columns together.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:114
#, no-wrap
msgid "So let us now talk about those connections. \n"
"What the implications for the integrity and security of your data of loose unenforced relationships between rows and columns?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:124
#, no-wrap
msgid "What actually makes a table useful are the connections that we create to give the data in the structure meaning. \n"
"We intrinsically make patterns of attributes that turns data into information. \n"
"Rows become records and Columns become attributes. \n"
"In a spreadsheet however, the connections between cells are idiomatic, loose and un-enforced. \n"
"It is easy for the attributes between the rows to get jumbled up or for the values get changed and then the meaning is lost.\n"
"`\n"
"// Slide 20\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:130
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lack of enforced connections makes a spreadsheet easy to use but these same qualities also cause problems. \n"
"Errors are easy to make and hard to catch -- Connections and relationships are not documentable and so subject to interpretation and mutation. \n"
"Spreadsheets are not built to scale either in terms of size or numbers of users -- So can be slow, crashy and easily corruptible. \n"
"Spreadsheets are easy to copy and email -- So they are hard to version and keep track of.\n"
"The question is what is the solution?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:141
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let us look back, at the connections in a bit more detail. \n"
"We generally understand that the data in each row of a table means something. \n"
"For example each row can represent a specimen or a taxa that was found at a particular place, at a particular time, by a particular person. \n"
"It would be odd to have one row in a table that represents a specimen followed by a row that represents a collection site. \n"
"We also often want to analyse, sort and compare values of particular attributes between rows. \n"
"For example you may want find the mean weight of all the Kenyan bats. \n"
"So ideally we want to use a system to store our data that: 1) always holds the rows together; 2)does not mix data types; 3) and segregates data in each table to a single concept or theme. \n"
"These are functions of a database!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:148
#, no-wrap
msgid "Functions in a true database inherently bind rows together and columns may only have a single data type. \n"
"Tables in database are held together by virtual links called keys. \n"
"These keys are identifiers that match between tables and allow data to be connected efficiently. \n"
"It is these 2 key concepts that distinguish a spreadsheet from a database.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:153
#, no-wrap
msgid "In review then, we have looked at the 6 most common data types, the basic building blocks of data structure and distinguished between a spreadsheet grid and database table.\n"
"Understanding these elements and how they interact with one another will allow you to better determine the best ways to manipulate and document the data that falls under your custodianship.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: scripts/structures.en.txt:159
#, no-wrap
msgid "Remember that: When you realize the difference between the container and the content, you will have knowledge. \n"
"Using the correct terms to describe both your data and the structures that you create and use to store it; is the most important way to ensure that it remains relevant and useable.\n"
"Never underestimate how much damage can be done to priceless information by careless descriptions and storage.\n"
msgstr ""

